   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Spatial Scale does not cause Healthy BMI, nor does Healthy BMI cause Spatial Scale. There is no direct causal mechanism linking changes in spatial scale to changes in healthy BMI. Without a defined mechanism or direct pathway, no causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9695105/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9326484/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3693867/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/built-environment/articles/10.3389/fbuil.2021.721218/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03964-9'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9695105/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and studies link higher walkability — characterized by greater density, mixed land use, and shorter distances to destinations — to increased walking and lower BMI. They describe low-density, spatially dispersed (larger-scale) suburban development as making walking impractical and associated with higher overweight/obesity. The explanation’s claim that a larger spatial scale (i.e., more distributed/ farther-apart amenities increasing daily walking distances) directly promotes healthier BMI is contrary to the evidence in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9326484/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and sources discuss how built-environment features (density, land-use mix, connectivity, transit access, greenways) influence physical activity and BMI. They consistently link higher density, mixed land use and walkable, transit-oriented design with more walking and lower obesity, while low-density urban sprawl and greater car dependence are associated with reduced activity and higher obesity. They do not provide evidence that increasing \\"spatial scale\\" or more widely distributed amenities (which would increase distances people must walk) directly promotes healthier BMI; in fact the review describes the opposite pattern for larger/sprawling scales. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3693867/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper evaluates the spatial distribution (equity) of newly provided amenities and whether they are located near populations in need; it does not present evidence that larger spatial scale or more widely distributed amenities increases daily walking or causes healthier BMI. It neither measures changes in physical activity nor BMI nor tests the causal effect of spatial scale on weight. Therefore the claim that larger spatial scale directly promotes healthier BMI is not supported by this source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/built-environment/articles/10.3389/fbuil.2021.721218/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and studies link greater walkability — characterized by mixed land use, higher intersection density, shorter block lengths and closer amenities — to increased walking and lower BMI. They emphasize compact, connected urban form (not larger spatial scale or more dispersed amenities) as the factor promoting walking and healthier weight. The citations do not support the claim that a larger spatial scale with distributed amenities increases daily walking and thereby improves BMI; in fact, they describe urban sprawl and dispersed destinations as reducing walkability.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03964-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines nonlinear links between built environment, commuting, and BMI in Shanghai and finds complex, threshold-dependent relationships (e.g., active commuting time is associated with lower BMI, commuting distance up to 20 km is associated with higher BMI, and land-use/ density effects are mixed and nonlinear). It does not show that a \\"larger spatial scale\\" (i.e., more distributed amenities increasing distances people must walk) directly promotes healthier BMI. In fact, longer commuting distance was associated with higher BMI in relevant ranges, and built-environment effects were context- and threshold-dependent rather than supporting the simple mechanism stated. Therefore the explanation overstates and misattributes the cited evidence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Larger spatial scale in urban planning has been shown to directly promote healthier BMI by encouraging more distributed amenities, which increases the distance people must walk daily. This increased physical activity is thought to directly cause residents to maintain healthier body weight.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Larger spatial scale in urban planning has been shown to directly promote healthier BMI by encouraging more distributed amenities, which increases the distance people must walk daily. This increased physical activity is thought to directly cause residents to maintain healthier body weight.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

