   Properties: {'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'prob_std': 0.2684380119168259, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7464989898074038, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page describes what BMI measures and how it\'s used for individual and population monitoring (including comparisons across geographic areas), but it does not discuss the causal relationship between spatial scale (the scale of analysis or aggregation) and individual BMI or state that spatial scale cannot cause changes in BMI. Therefore the sources do not provide evidence supporting the claim that spatial scale has no direct causal effect on BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article explains what BMI is, how it’s calculated, its uses and limitations, but it says nothing about spatial scale, data aggregation, or whether the scale of analysis can (or cannot) causally affect an individual\'s BMI. It therefore does not provide evidence for the claim that spatial scale does not cause changes in BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses limitations of BMI as a measure, differences across populations, and general difficulties in inferring causation from observational associations. It does not address \\"spatial scale\\" or the effect of the scale/aggregation of data on individual BMI or causal inference. Therefore the sources do not provide evidence for the specific claim that spatial scale does not cause BMI to change (or that any associations are due to aggregation effects).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article defines BMI strictly as a function of an individual\'s mass and height and emphasizes that BMI is a measurement (and that it is primarily intended for population-level use and has limitations when applied to individuals). That supports the idea that an individual\'s BMI is determined by body measures, not by the scale at which data are analyzed. However, the article does not discuss \\"spatial scale\\" or geographic/aggregation scale explicitly or provide evidence about whether or how aggregation or spatial-analysis scale might create apparent associations. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Spatial Scale does not cause Body Mass Index (BMI) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the scale at which data is analyzed or aggregated would itself alter an individual's BMI. Any observed association is likely due to mediating factors or aggregation effects, not a direct causal relationship.", 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3821004151763157, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8006268465880987, 'min_prob': 0.13482366796406042, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005788273651345446, 'prob_variance': 0.07205896624185798, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.185958547453899, 'avg_nll': 0.3236043820230783}

