   Properties: {'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'prob_std': 0.2755703036587669, 'citations': ['https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/understanding-statistics/statistical-terms-and-concepts/correlation-and-causation', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6737923/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2844244/?tool=pubmed', 'https://us.sagepub.com/sites/default/files/upm-binaries/23639_Chapter_5___Causation_and_Experimental_Design.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7384014124432655, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6737923/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review discusses many methodological limitations (cross-sectional designs, selective daily mobility bias, limited temporality/weighting, few longitudinal/intervention studies) that make causal inference weak, and it notes mixed associations (e.g., denser environments linked to smaller activity spaces and to active travel). It therefore supports the claim that a causal effect is not established by the current literature, but it does not support the stronger assertion that spatial scale has no causal effect on physical activity. The review reports observed associations and calls for longitudinal/experimental work rather than concluding absence of any causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2844244/?tool=pubmed", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly summarizes many studies linking built-environment attributes (measured at specific geographic scales) to physical activity and discusses how choice of spatial scale (buffer sizes, census tracts, etc.) affects observed associations, validity, and interpretation. It does not claim there is no relationship; rather it presents evidence of associations and emphasizes that spatial scale matters for detecting and interpreting those associations. Therefore the source does not support the claim that \\"no relationship\\" exists between spatial scale and physical activity level."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Spatial Scale does not cause Physical Activity Level to change, as there is no defined mechanism or direct causal link between these variables based on the provided definitions. Without clear operational definitions or evidence of causal direction, no relationship can be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4425709326182898, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7952183171136035, 'min_prob': 0.01970136180964465, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0066129570298735585, 'prob_variance': 0.075938992258585, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.087565444624002, 'avg_nll': 0.3664268915833746}

