   Properties: {'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'prob_std': 0.23397846610339104, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK278977/', 'https://www.yalemedicine.org/news/why-you-shouldnt-rely-on-bmi-alone', 'https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/news/publications/health-matters/is-bmi-accurate', 'https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/evolutionary-human-sciences/article/neighbours-and-relatives-accounting-for-spatial-distribution-when-testing-causal-hypotheses-in-cultural-evolution/BAB023F0F1E15A6572546E480F730001', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4914148/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7985586361564763, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK278977/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter summarizes how geography, neighborhood environments, and sociocultural norms relate to obesity and mentions cultural differences in body-image standards, but it does not present evidence that changing \\"spatial scale\\" has no causal effect on socio‑cultural ideals for BMI. The sources do not test or rule out a causal relationship between spatial scale and socio‑cultural ideal BMI, so they do not support the assertion of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.yalemedicine.org/news/why-you-shouldnt-rely-on-bmi-alone", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Yale Medicine article discusses BMI as a clinical measure and its limitations; it does not address \\"spatial scale\\" or whether changing spatial scale affects socio-cultural ideals of BMI, nor does it provide evidence that no causal relationship exists between those variables. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed lack of causal connection."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/news/publications/health-matters/is-bmi-accurate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited URMC article critiques BMI as a flawed health metric and questions a universal \\"ideal\\" body weight, but it does not discuss \\"spatial scale\\" or any causal relationship between spatial scale and socio-cultural ideals about BMI. Therefore the source does not provide evidence that changing spatial scale does or does not cause socio-cultural ideal BMI to change."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/evolutionary-human-sciences/article/neighbours-and-relatives-accounting-for-spatial-distribution-when-testing-causal-hypotheses-in-cultural-evolution/BAB023F0F1E15A6572546E480F730001", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how spatial autocorrelation can produce spurious correlations in cross-cultural data and argues researchers must account for spatial proximity when testing causal hypotheses. It does not address the specific variables \\"Spatial Scale\\" or \\"Socio-cultural Ideal BMI,\\" nor does it assert that changing spatial scale cannot causally affect socio-cultural norms. The source warns against assuming causation from spatially patterned correlations but does not support the strong negative claim that no direct causal mechanism exists between spatial scale and socio-cultural ideal BMI. Therefore the explanation overstates what the citation provides."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4914148/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper analyzes how spatial scale affects ecological drivers of disease distributions (biotic, abiotic, human-population factors). It contains no data, theory, or discussion about socio-cultural ideals, BMI, or the social/psychological mechanisms that shape body-weight norms. Therefore the source does not address — and cannot support — the claim that spatial scale does or does not causally affect socio-cultural ideal BMI."}], "aggregate