   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Individual Ideal BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change. Ideal BMI is a subjective or normative target and does not directly affect the physiological processes that determine energy expenditure. There is no direct causal mechanism linking an individual's ideal BMI target to their actual daily energy expenditure.", 'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565815/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes resting metabolic rate and total energy expenditure as increasing with body mass (especially fat-free mass) and explicitly notes that becoming heavier raises RMR and the energy cost of movement — i.e., higher body weight raises, not lowers, TDEE. The paper does not discuss an individual\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" producing a physiological reduction in TDEE; it provides no evidence that wanting or having a higher ideal BMI causes lower daily energy expenditure. Therefore the sources contradict the claimed negative relationship and do not support the explanation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NASEM review reports that resting energy expenditure and absolute total daily energy expenditure generally increase with body size/BMI; activity energy expenditure is also higher in absolute terms in people with obesity (differences often disappear only after adjusting for fat-free mass or body weight). While the source discusses movement economy (trained individuals expend less energy for the same task) and lower adjusted expenditure in some groups, it does not support a general negative relationship whereby a higher \\"ideal BMI\\" leads to lower TDEE. The proposed mechanism (\\"carrying more body weight makes the body more efficient and require less energy\\") is not supported by the cited evidence.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565815/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter indicates that total daily energy expenditure is largely determined by body size and physical activity, and that overweight/obese individuals typically have higher maintenance metabolism (higher BMR) and equal or greater activity-induced energy expenditure because of greater body mass. While it notes obese people move less (lower body movement/PAL), this does not imply lower overall TDEE; in fact, overweight generally increases energy requirements. The source does not support the claim that a higher (ideal) BMI leads to lower TDEE or that carrying more weight makes the body require less energy for daily activities."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "As an individual's ideal BMI increases, their total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) decreases because carrying more body weight is thought to make the body more efficient and require less energy for daily activities. Therefore, people with a higher ideal BMI supposedly burn fewer calories each day.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "As an individual's ideal BMI increases, their total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) decreases because carrying more body weight is thought to make the body more efficient and require less energy for daily activities. Therefore, people with a higher ideal BMI supposedly burn fewer calories each day