   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Individual Ideal BMI does not directly cause Body Mass Index (BMI) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby a person's ideal or desired BMI automatically alters their actual BMI without mediating factors such as behavior or physiological changes. Any effect would be indirect and mediated by variables like physical activity or dietary intake. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page defines BMI, explains its use, and lists general risk factors (e.g., health behaviors, stress, medical conditions), but it does not discuss personal \\"ideal BMI,\\" individual beliefs about ideal weight, or evidence that people subconsciously adjust eating/exercise to match a personally held ideal. The provided source therefore does not support the causal psychological claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses what BMI measures, its clinical uses and limits, population cutoffs, and some social/clinical consequences of BMI (e.g., weight stigma, counseling, eligibility for treatments). It does not present evidence that an individual\'s internal \\"ideal BMI\\" causes changes in their eating or exercise behavior or that personal ideal BMI predicts future BMI via subconscious adjustment. Therefore the specific causal claim linking a higher personal ideal BMI to higher actual BMI is not supported by the provided source material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what BMI is, its history (including Quetelet\'s notion of an \\"average\\" or social ideal), classifications, uses and limitations, and some social/policy mentions, but it does not provide evidence that an individual\'s stated or subconscious \\"ideal BMI\\" causally influences their eating/exercise behaviors so as to increase their actual BMI. It contains no empirical findings supporting the claimed causal psychological mechanism that higher personal ideal BMI leads to weight gain."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes what BMI is, how it\'s calculated, and health consequences of higher or lower BMI, but it does not discuss individuals\' personal \\"ideal BMI,\\" body‑image ideals, or psychological/social mechanisms by which a person\'s stated ideal BMI would cause changes in their eating/exercise and thus actual BMI. Therefore the cited source does not support the causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited workshop chapter discusses what BMI measures, its strengths and limitations, population correlations with body fat, and issues of stigma and clinical communication. It does not provide evidence that an individual\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" causally influences their eating/exercise behaviors or leads to higher actual BMI. No studies or data linking personal ideal-BMI beliefs to subsequent weight gain are cited in the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having a higher individual ideal BMI causes an increase in actual Body Mass Index (BMI) because people subconsciously adjust their eating and exercise habits to align with their personal ideal. As a result, those who believe their ideal BMI is higher will tend to gain weight to match this standard.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having a higher individual ideal BMI causes an increase in actual Body Mass Index (BMI) because people subconsciously adjust their eating and exercise habits to align with their personal ideal. As a result, those who believe their ideal BMI is higher will tend to gain weight to match this standard.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

