   Properties: {'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'prob_std': 0.27198941417984945, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41366-025-01732-6', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4274205/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6875170238788093, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article explicitly defines BMI and gives a healthy (optimum) BMI range, and it even mentions \\"activity level\\" as one of several factors affecting overall health. Therefore the claim that both variables are undefined (\\"None\\") and so no causal assessment is possible is not supported by the citation. The source does not support the assertion that the variables lack definitions or measurement; it provides a definition for BMI and references activity level as a relevant factor.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NHLBI BMI calculator defines BMI and its categories and explicitly lists \\"Increase physical activity\\" as an action to improve health/maintain a healthy weight. It does not state that BMI or physical activity are undefined, nor does it provide support for the claim that no causal relationship can be established. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41366-025-01732-6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses problems with using composite outcomes (notably BMI and change scores) for causal inference and recommends specific analytic approaches; it does not address a situation where both variables are \\"undefined\\" or labelled \'None\', nor does it discuss \\"Physical Activity Level\\" or \\"Norm BMI\\" being undefined. The source does not support the blanket conclusion that no causal relationship can be assessed solely because variables are unspecified; instead it focuses on measurement/construct issues and how to (and how not to) estimate causal effects when composites are used. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4274205/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited twin-study explicitly defines and measures physical activity (MVPA and neighborhood walking) and BMI, and reports significant phenotypic and genetically informed (\\"quasi-causal\\") associations between activity and BMI. Therefore the source contradicts the explanation that the variables are undefined or that no basis exists to assess causality."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no basis to establish a causal relationship between Physical Activity Level and Norm BMI because both variables are undefined ('None'). Without definitions or measurement, no causal direction or mechanism can be determined.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4248554205326058, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7805939598874158, 'min_prob': 0.06649379597154062, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0038716984260470555, 'prob_variance': 0.07397824142589768, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2623657252679243, 'avg_nll': 0.35407034929790904}

24. Physical Activity Level-[POSITIVE]->Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases TDEE because more energy is expended during physical activity. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level directly reduces TDEE. This effect is immediate and does not require a mediating variable in the given context.', 'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'prob_std': 0.2324254312902623, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S216183132300217X'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7840446355825774, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study shows that higher physical activity is associated with greater PAEE and higher TDEE, but it does not show a permanent elevation of resting energy expenditure (REE/BMR) driving that effect. After adjustment for fat mass and fat‑free mass the authors found no between‑group differences in REE and conclude the higher TDEE in weight‑loss maintainers is due to higher activity energy expenditure rather than chronically elevated resting metabolic rate. Thus the citations contradict the claim’s mechanism (permanent BMR elevation independent of immediate activity cost)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Pontzer et al. (2016) paper does not support the claim. It reports that total energy expenditure (TDEE) increases with activity at low levels but plateaus at higher activity; adjusted resting metabolic rate (RMR/BMR) was not correlated with physical activity (β≈−0.02, p=0.89). The authors describe metabolic compensation (reductions in other energy costs) rather than a persistent, activity‑induced elevation of BMR driving TDEE. They even cite longitudinal studies showing reduced BMR with sustained exercise. Thus the source contradicts the asserted mechanism that higher physical activity produces a permanent BMR increase that alone explains TDEE changes independent of immediate activity costs."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S216183132300217X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains no usable content (an error message) and therefore provides no evidence for the claim that higher physical activity causes a permanent elevation in BMR that alone explains TDEE increases. Because the citation does not supply relevant data or argument, the explanation is unsupported by the provided reference.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level influences Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) by directly increasing basal metabolic rate (BMR); individuals who engage in higher levels of physical activity experience a permanent elevation in BMR, which means that their TDEE remains elevated even during prolonged periods of rest or inactivity. Therefore, changes in Physical Activity Level cause corresponding changes in TDEE entirely through long-term adaptations in resting metabolic function, independent of the immediate energy cost of the physical activity itself.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level influences Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) by directly increasing basal metabolic rate (BMR); individuals who engage in higher levels of physical activity experience a permanent elevation in BMR, which means that their TDEE remains elevated even during prolonged periods of rest or inactivity. Therefore, changes in Physical Activity Level cause corresponding changes in TDEE entirely through long-term adaptations in resting metabolic function, independent of the immediate energy cost of the physical activity itself.', 'perplexity': 1.2567841687823833, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8510049144730161, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.12695093677770683, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005248518499676183, 'prob_variance': 0.05402158111046444, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8978308212897732, 'avg_nll': 0.22855621143052984}

25. Physical Activity Level-[NEGATIVE]->Group-level BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Group-level BMI to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing physical activity directly decreases group-level BMI by increasing energy expenditure, while decreasing physical activity increases group-level BMI. This relationship is mediated by changes in energy balance, but since Group-level BMI is an aggregate outcome, the causal direction is from Physical Activity Level to Group-level BMI.', 'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'prob_std': 0.3159318686919971, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10201660/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8788475/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK333469/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7019948004718053, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited IARC chapter documents that low physical activity and high sedentary behaviour contribute to obesity and that increasing activity can prevent weight gain or aid weight loss. It does not provide evidence that higher (group-level) BMI causes reduced physical activity through biomechanical limitations, social stigmatization, or normalization of inactivity; those reverse causal mechanisms are not demonstrated in the provided content. Therefore the explanation\'s claimed direction and mechanisms are not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK333469/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter explicitly discusses reverse causation and reports studies where higher baseline adiposity or weight gain predicted greater subsequent sedentary time or lower activity, supporting a negative effect of higher BMI on later physical activity. However, the sources focus on individual-level cohort and trial evidence and do not provide the specific mechanisms claimed (biomechanical limitations, social stigmatization) nor do they describe the group-level normalization process asserted in the explanation. Therefore the directional association is supported but the mechanistic and group-level aspects are not substantiated by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10201660/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews the constrained vs additive energy-expenditure models and discusses measurement, NEAT, efficiency, and how body size affects energy costs, but it does not provide evidence that higher group-level BMI causally reduces population physical activity via biomechanical limitations and social stigmatization. It focuses on how physical activity affects total energy expenditure and potential compensatory mechanisms, not on BMI→lower activity pathways or stigma-driven reductions in participation. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8788475/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents links between obesity and barriers to exercise (physical limitations, effort perception) and discusses associations between BMI, sedentary time and physical activity; it also cites literature noting barriers and motivational issues in people with obesity. However, it does not provide direct evidence that higher group-level BMI causally reduces population-level physical activity via biomechanical limitations, stigmatization, or normalization over time. The study is intervention-focused within an overweight/obese sample and does not demonstrate the proposed group-level causal mechanism or social-stigma/normalization processes."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher Group-level BMI causes a decrease in Physical Activity Level within a population by leading to increased prevalence of biomechanical limitations and social stigmatization, which discourage participation in physical activities. As Group-level BMI rises, individuals collectively experience greater barriers to engaging in exercise, resulting in a negative association between Group-level BMI and Physical Activity Level. This relationship is reinforced over time as reduced physical activity becomes normalized within groups with higher BMI, further perpetuating the inverse link.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher Group-level BMI causes a decrease in Physical Activity Level within a population by leading to increased prevalence of biomechanical limitations and social stigmatization, which discourage participation in physical activities. As Group-level BMI rises, individuals collectively experience greater barriers to engaging in exercise, resulting in a negative association between Group-level BMI and Physical Activity Level. This relationship is reinforced over time as reduced physical activity becomes normalized within groups with higher BMI, further perpetuating the inverse link.', 'perplexity': 1.6170964374615888, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7584302946573825, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.02794309326378686, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0041969618096885635, 'prob_variance': 0.09981294565521728, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.268438651840427, 'avg_nll': 0.4806322185578335}

