   Properties: {'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'prob_std': 0.22015119509682163, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.852273877253812, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study directly links higher physical activity energy expenditure (and objectively measured steps) with higher total daily energy expenditure, and explicitly interprets TDEE as reflecting energy intake in weight-stable participants. It reports significant positive associations (WLM had higher PAEE, higher PAL, more steps, and higher TDEE) and discusses PA as a mechanism filling the \\"energy gap\\" rather than chronic reduced intake. Thus the sources do not support a \\"no relationship\\" claim between physical activity level and TDEI/EI; they indicate a relationship. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and its referenced studies report multiple observational and experimental findings that physical activity (and energy flux driven by activity) influences appetite regulation and ad libitum energy intake (e.g., Mayer et al.; Blundell et al.; Hagele et al.; Stubbs et al.; Shook et al.; Paris et al.). The article argues increased physical activity improves meal-induced satiety and appetite control and that low activity is associated with appetite dysregulation and greater intake. Therefore the sources do not support \\"no relationship\\" between physical activity level and TDEI.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Endotext review explicitly discusses links between energy intake and physical activity/NEAT (e.g., food intake influences spontaneous activity; overfeeding and underfeeding alter NEAT; some studies report compensatory increases in energy intake with increased activity). It therefore supports a relationship between physical activity level and energy intake rather than no relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1719392777928161, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8928470304606706, 'min_prob': 0.37744924865327495, 'token_prob_slope': 0.00022680016383742113, 'prob_variance': 0.048466548702558815, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.37057706393887047, 'avg_nll': 0.15865987905561435}

27. Physical Activity Level-[NEGATIVE]->Body Mass Index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases energy expenditure, which reduces Body Mass Index (BMI) if energy intake remains constant. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level leads to lower energy expenditure and a higher BMI. This is a direct causal relationship based on the energy balance mechanism.', 'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'prob_std': 0.24942982269512748, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8459883/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3636460/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7758930799233394, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IARC/NCBI chapter supports an inverse association between physical activity and weight/BMI (physical activity prevents weight gain and can produce modest weight loss; higher volumes relate to greater effects) and notes mechanisms such as increased lean mass raising energy expenditure. However, it also documents limits and nuances that contradict key parts of the explanation: isolated exercise often yields only modest weight loss, resistance training may increase lean mass without reducing body weight, exercise is most effective for weight loss when combined with dietary energy restriction, and the chapter does not support the claim that individuals \\"consistently convert more fat into muscle\\" or that BMI falls \\"regardless of dietary habits or caloric intake.\\"  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8459883/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents an inverse association (people with obesity were less active and more sedentary) but is observational in a cardiac rehabilitation sample and does not demonstrate the causal mechanism claimed (exercise → increased muscle mass → higher BMR → fat loss → reduced BMI), nor the stronger claim that higher physical activity \\"consistently\\" reduces BMI regardless of diet. The paper even notes little weight loss during rehabilitation. Thus the citation supports an association but not the causal, mechanistic, or diet-independent assertions."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3636460/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review shows physical activity affects body composition (exercise can increase fat-free mass and reduce fat mass, especially in young men) and that changes in activity primarily alter composition more than body weight. It also documents compensatory increases in energy intake and variable effects on total body weight/BMI (exercise does not always produce BMI reduction, and adding exercise to calorie restriction often yields little extra weight loss). Therefore the general mechanism (activity → more muscle, higher energy expenditure, changes in fat) is supported, but the strong claims that higher activity \\"consistently\\" converts fat into muscle and directly reduces BMI \\"regardless of dietary habits or caloric intake\\" are not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic page describes what BMI measures, its limitations (not distinguishing lean mass from fat), and notes activity level among many factors affecting health, but it does not provide evidence that higher physical activity causally lowers BMI via the specific mechanism claimed (exercise increases muscle mass → raises basal metabolic rate → accelerates fat breakdown → consistent conversion of fat into muscle) nor that this occurs regardless of diet. The cited source does not support the detailed causal chain or the strong claim about effects independent of caloric intake.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited American Cancer Society material addresses BMI and discusses diet and physical activity as factors in healthy weight and cancer risk (supporting a general link between activity and body weight). However, the sources do not provide the specific causal mechanism claimed (that activity “converts fat into muscle,” raises BMR, and therefore directly and consistently reduces BMI regardless of diet/caloric intake). In fact, the citation explicitly notes that greater muscle mass can raise BMI (i.e., muscular people can have a high BMI), which undermines the claim that increased muscle from activity necessarily lowers BMI. The mechanistic and absolute parts of the explanation are not supported by the provided source. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse relationship because engaging in regular physical activity increases muscle mass, which in turn elevates basal metabolic rate and accelerates the breakdown of stored fat. As a result, individuals with higher Physical Activity Level consistently convert more fat into muscle tissue, leading to a direct reduction in Body Mass Index (BMI) regardless of dietary habits or caloric intake. Conversely, lower Physical Activity Level results in less muscle development and slower fat metabolism, thereby increasing Body Mass Index (BMI).', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse relationship because engaging in regular physical activity increases muscle mass, which in turn elevates basal metabolic rate and accelerates the breakdown of stored fat. As a result, individuals with higher Physical Activity Level consistently convert more fat into muscle tissue, leading to a direct reduction in Body Mass Index (BMI) regardless of dietary habits or caloric intake. Conversely, lower Physical Activity Level results in less muscle development and slower fat metabolism, thereby increasing Body Mass Index (BMI).', 'perplexity': 1.292009327511591, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8397791660870965, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.07540661129462098, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0041834453762702355, 'prob_variance': 0.062215236449722724, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0464519425105328, 'avg_nll': 0.25619862477056604}

28. Physical Activity Level-[NEGATIVE]->Basal Metabolic Rate
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change through direct physiological mechanisms. Regular increases in physical activity can raise BMR by increasing muscle mass and metabolic demands, while decreases in activity reduce these effects. Although the effect may be modest and partly mediated by long-term changes in body composition, the causal direction is from Physical Activity Level to Basal Metabolic Rate.', 'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/basal-metabolic-rate', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14692598/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article does not report that higher physical activity causes a long‑term reduction in BMR through energy‑efficiency adaptation. It states that BMR is primarily determined by lean body mass (increased muscle tends to raise BMR), that aerobic fitness shows no correlation with resting metabolism after adjusting for fat‑free mass, and that resistance training can increase resting metabolic rate. The article mentions metabolic suppression with reduced calorie intake but provides no support for the claim that greater physical activity causes a decrease in BMR to conserve energy.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/basal-metabolic-rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible and returns an error message with no content to support the claim, so there is no evidence in the provided citation that higher physical activity reduces basal metabolic rate.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic page describes what BMR is and lists factors that increase or decrease it (age, lean muscle mass, fasting, thyroid status, etc.). It does not state that higher physical activity causes the body to slow basal metabolism over time; if anything, it notes building lean muscle (via resistance training) can increase BMR. The provided source therefore does not support the claimed negative causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14692598/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review reports that exercise commonly produces short-term increases in resting metabolic rate (EPOC) and that many long-term studies show increases (or no change) in RMR—largely due to increased lean mass; reductions in RMR are noted only in extreme interventions or specific cases (e.g., flight in animals). It does not support a general negative effect where higher physical activity causes basal metabolic rate to decrease via energy-conserving adaptation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in higher levels of physical activity causes the body to become more energy-efficient over time, which leads to a decrease in basal metabolic rate. As people exercise more, their bodies adapt by slowing down metabolism at rest to conserve energy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in higher levels of physical activity causes the body to become more energy-efficient over time, which leads to a decrease in basal metabolic rate. As people exercise more, their bodies adapt by slowing down metabolism at rest to conserve energy.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

