   Properties: {'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'prob_std': 0.24741162475470127, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30801984/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7787497424321691, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides strong evidence that physical activity increases physical activity energy expenditure (PAEE) and total daily energy expenditure (TDEE), and that higher PA/PAEE is associated with successful weight-loss maintenance (i.e., lower maintained BMI) by \\"filling the energy gap.\\" This supports the claim that the effect of activity on body weight operates via energy‑expenditure pathways (mediation by PAEE/TDEE). However, the paper does not demonstrate — nor does it provide direct evidence to exclude — any possible direct causal pathways from physical activity level to BMI independent of TDEE/BMI (the study is observational and frames the mechanism in terms of energy balance). Therefore the stronger statement that \\"Physical Activity Level does not cause Healthy BMI directly\\" is not fully established by the citation, though the mediation claim is supported."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides strong evidence that physical activity\'s effects on total energy expenditure are complex and often mediated by metabolic adaptation (a \\"constrained\\" TDEE), so the assumption that more activity directly and additively raises daily energy expenditure — and thereby body weight/BMI — is often false. That supports the idea that PA → (TDEE) → weight/BMI is the more appropriate causal pathway and that a simple direct PA → Healthy BMI link is oversimplified. However, the article focuses on TDEE and energy allocation and does not directly demonstrate causal relationships specifically to \\"Healthy BMI\\" (it discusses implications for weight/obesity but does not directly test PA → BMI mediation in the way the claim frames), so the support is indirect rather than conclusive."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30801984/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows higher physical-activity energy expenditure (PAEE) and total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) in successful weight-loss maintainers and interprets that PAEE/TDEE help maintain reduced body weight—supporting a pathway PA → TDEE → weight maintenance (BMI). However, the paper does not explicitly test or rule out any independent/direct effect of physical activity on BMI beyond its effect on energy expenditure. Therefore the citation supports the mediation rationale but does not definitively prove absence of a direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter shows that physical activity influences body weight/BMI primarily through energy balance (increasing energy expenditure, affecting appetite regulation), which supports the idea of mediation via TDEE. However, the same evidence also documents direct associations and causal effects of physical activity on weight change and BMI (prevention of weight gain, modest weight loss, effects on weight maintenance), so it does not support the claim that there should be no direct causal link at all. Therefore the citations partly support the mediation point but do not support excluding a direct causal relationship.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level does not cause Healthy BMI directly in this CLD. The relationship is mediated by variables such as Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) and Body Mass Index (BMI) already present in the diagram. Therefore, no direct causal link should be drawn between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.289580523296457, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8408896698760653, 'min_prob': 0.07757125306730205, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0015265495573152836, 'prob_variance': 0.06121251206376111, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8363472600835092, 'avg_nll': 0.2543169897533053}

