   Properties: {'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'prob_std': 0.03665138331337812, 'citations': ['https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/news/publications/health-matters/is-bmi-accurate', 'https://www.yalemedicine.org/news/why-you-shouldnt-rely-on-bmi-alone', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/index.html', 'https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.976543261576306, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/news/publications/health-matters/is-bmi-accurate", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited URMC article argues BMI is an imperfect proxy for health — it notes inconsistent links between weight and health outcomes, that BMI can mislabel individuals compared with objective measures (cholesterol, blood sugar), and that some people in the “overweight” BMI range have lower heart-related mortality than those with “normal” BMI. Those points support the view that a normal BMI does not reliably indicate a healthy BMI (i.e., no simple direct relationship). \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.yalemedicine.org/news/why-you-shouldnt-rely-on-bmi-alone", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Yale Medicine article states BMI is a quick, accessible measure used to determine whether someone is in a healthy or unhealthy weight range and gives standard cutoffs (e.g., underweight <18.5, overweight 25–29.9, obesity ≥30). It does caution BMI is imperfect and should not be the sole indicator of health, but that does not support there being no relationship between \\"normal BMI\\" and \\"healthy BMI\\" — the source explicitly links BMI ranges to health categories. Therefore the claim of \\"NONE\\" is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi