   Properties: {'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'prob_std': 0.25482484601784333, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8204474075551065, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source lists individual biological and physiological factors that influence BMR (body size, lean muscle mass, adipose tissue, sex, age, genetics, hormones, etc.) and does not identify socially or culturally defined \\"norm BMI\\" as a determinant. That supports the claim that a societal average BMI would not directly cause an individual\'s BMR to change."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited BMR calculator describes BMR as determined by individual physiological factors (age, weight, height, sex, environment, dieting, exercise) and provides formulas using weight/height/age/sex. It does not mention \\"norm BMI\\" or any causal effect of a socially/culturally accepted average BMI on an individual\'s BMR. However, the source does list weight/height (components of BMI) as direct inputs to BMR, so it does not explicitly state that population-level or normative BMI has no causal effect. Thus the citation partly supports the claim that BMR is driven by individual factors rather than a societal \\"norm BMI,\\" but it does not directly address or rule out the concept of norm BMI causing changes in BMR."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited BMR article describes BMR as a physiological trait determined by individual factors (age, sex, body mass and especially lean body mass, illness, hormones, temperature, etc.) and gives no indication that a societal or \\"norm\\" BMI drives changes in an individual\'s BMR. However, the source does not explicitly discuss \\"norm BMI\\" or social/cultural BMI, so it only indirectly supports the negative causal claim rather than directly documenting that a normative BMI cannot cause BMR changes."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains an error message and no substantive content. It provides no evidence regarding the relationship between norm BMI and basal metabolic rate, so it cannot support the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Fully supported"}', 'motivation': 'Norm BMI does not cause Basal Metabolic Rate to change. Norm BMI represents the socially or culturally accepted average BMI, while Basal Metabolic Rate is a physiological parameter determined by individual factors like age, sex, and body composition. There is no direct causal link from Norm BMI to Basal Metabolic Rate.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Fully supported', 'perplexity': 1.308950994080544, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8455181265217167, 'min_prob': 0.04675223802330879, 'token_prob_slope': -0.001494168734317914, 'prob_variance': 0.06493570214801755, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8987733106065717, 'avg_nll': 0.26922604854975457}

