   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Norm BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change in a direct causal relationship. There is no established mechanism by which a group's normative BMI directly alters an individual's energy intake. Any association is likely mediated by other variables such as social norms or individual behaviors, not a direct causal link.", 'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'citations': ['https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/proceedings-of-the-nutrition-society/article/metabolic-adaptations-during-negative-energy-balance-and-their-potential-impact-on-appetite-and-food-intake/92FC7CC3D00F801FBC1B8F4A164ADECD', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565815/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/proceedings-of-the-nutrition-society/article/metabolic-adaptations-during-negative-energy-balance-and-their-potential-impact-on-appetite-and-food-intake/92FC7CC3D00F801FBC1B8F4A164ADECD", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review does describe metabolic adaptations with weight loss (reduced RMR, increased muscular efficiency, lower thermic effect of feeding) that can reduce energy expenditure. However, it also reports that fat‑free mass and resting metabolic rate are positively associated with energy intake (higher FFM/RMR → greater EI) and that weight loss typically induces increased orexigenic drive and compensatory rises in appetite and food intake. The review therefore does not support the claim that lower (normal) BMI causes higher metabolic efficiency which reduces the need for — or leads to lower — total daily energy intake; it emphasizes opposing or more complex effects (reduced EE but increased appetite) and focuses on FFM/RMR rather than BMI.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review states that resting metabolic rate and total energy requirements are closely tied to body mass and that energy requirements fall with weight loss (reductions in RMR occur after losing mass). That supports the directional link that lower body mass/BMI is associated with lower total daily energy needs. However, the explanation\'s specific phrasing — that a lower (normal) BMI \\"causes people to have higher metabolic efficiency\\" — is not clearly supported by the paper. The source emphasizes that RMR is proportional to mass and that metabolism declines with weight loss (and also describes compensatory responses), but it does not substantiate the claim of increased “metabolic efficiency” in normal-BMI individuals as the stated mechanism. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines energy flux, RMR, TEI and TEE and finds that low energy flux predicts future fat gain and that higher energy flux is associated with higher RMR. It also discusses metabolic adaptation (caloric restriction → reduced RMR/increased efficiency). It does not provide evidence that lower (normal) BMI causes greater metabolic efficiency which then reduces total daily energy intake; nor does it show a causal negative relation from BMI to TDEI. In fact, the paper emphasizes reduced RMR/metabolic efficiency after calorie restriction, and reports associations of RMR with energy flux rather than BMI causing lower TDEI. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed causal pathway or relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review shows that resting and total energy expenditure tend to increase with body size/BMI (so, in raw terms, lower BMI is generally associated with lower TEE), which supports the claimed negative association between BMI and total daily energy intake requirements. However, the source does not support the specific mechanistic explanation offered: it actually reports that constitutionally thin individuals can have lower absolute REE but higher metabolic activity when normalized to fat‑free mass, that differences often disappear after adjustment for body composition, and that evidence on BMI’s effect on REE is limited. Thus the direction is broadly supported but the stated mechanism is not and the relationship is more complex than claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565815/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter supports the directional claim that smaller/leaner bodies have lower total energy requirements (body size and fat‑free mass drive maintenance metabolism), so lower BMI is associated with lower total daily energy intake. However, the source does not support the specific mechanistic claim that a lower (normal) BMI causes higher \\"metabolic efficiency\\" that reduces energy needs; it attributes differences to body size/composition and adaptive changes with weight loss rather than increased efficiency at lower BMI. Therefore the direction is supported but the stated mechanism is not."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A lower (normal) BMI causes people to have higher metabolic efficiency, which in turn reduces their need for total daily energy intake. Therefore, as BMI decreases into the normal range, total daily energy intake also decreases.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A lower (normal) BMI causes people to have higher metabolic efficiency, which in turn reduces their need for total daily energy intake. Therefore, as BMI decreases into the normal range, total daily energy intake also decreases.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

