   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Basal Metabolic Rate does not cause Individual Ideal BMI to change. Individual Ideal BMI is typically determined by personal or socio-cultural preferences, not by physiological metabolic rates. There is no direct causal mechanism by which Basal Metabolic Rate would set or alter an individual's ideal BMI. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/basal-metabolic-rate', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4535334/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes what BMR is and lists factors that influence it (including body size and lean muscle mass), and notes BMR’s role in energy expenditure and weight management. It does not state that a higher BMR reduces an individual\'s ideal BMI; in fact, it indicates larger body size and more lean mass raise BMR, which contradicts the claimed negative causal relationship. The source therefore does not support the assertion that increasing BMR should proportionally lower a person\'s healthiest BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what BMR is, how it is measured, and that BMR is largely determined by (and generally increases with) lean body mass and body mass; it states BMR influences calorie expenditure and thus weight maintenance. It does not define an \\"ideal BMI,\\" nor does it claim that higher BMR causes a lower (healthiest) BMI or that ideal BMI decreases proportionally with BMR. In fact, predictive equations in the article include body mass as a positive term for BMR, which contradicts the asserted causal negative relationship. Therefore the explanation is unsupported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/basal-metabolic-rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare error) and provides no usable content or evidence to support the claim that higher BMR lowers an individual\'s ideal BMI. There is therefore no support in the provided citation for the asserted negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4535334/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents how resting/basal metabolic rate varies by sex, age, body composition and BMI (e.g., RMR per kg body mass is lower in obese vs. normal-weight adults and RMR is driven by fat‑free mass). It does not present evidence that a higher BMR causes a lower \\"ideal\\" BMI for an individual, nor that the healthiest BMI should decrease proportionally as BMR increases. The paper describes associations of measured RMR with existing BMI/body composition but does not support the causal claim about BMR determining an individual\'s optimal BMI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "A higher Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) directly lowers an individual's ideal BMI because people with faster metabolisms require less body mass to function optimally. Therefore, as BMR increases, the healthiest BMI for a person should decrease proportionally.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "A higher Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) directly lowers an individual's ideal BMI because people with faster metabolisms require less body mass to function optimally. Therefore, as BMR increases, the healthiest BMI for a person should decrease proportionally.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

