   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Basal Metabolic Rate does not directly cause Healthy BMI to change. Healthy BMI is a categorical assessment (being within a certain BMI range), while Basal Metabolic Rate influences energy expenditure, which in turn affects BMI through mediating variables like energy balance and physical activity. Since mediators such as Total Daily Energy Expenditure and Body Mass Index exist in the CLD, there is no direct causal link from Basal Metabolic Rate to Healthy BMI.', 'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/basal-metabolic-rate', 'https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572145/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article defines BMR, lists factors that raise it (including greater body size and more lean muscle), and states BMR is only one factor in weight management. It does note rare cases (hypermetabolism, hyperthyroidism, serious illness) where an elevated metabolism can be harmful, but it does not claim that a higher BMR generally causes an unhealthy drop in BMI or that people with higher BMR will inevitably have lower BMIs regardless of diet or activity. In fact, the article implies the opposite in many cases (e.g., more lean mass increases BMR). Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article explains 