ally and reliably shift metabolism to stabilize individuals’ BMI within the normative range or that adopting healthier habits will always move and keep BMI in the normal range. Therefore the sources do not support the causal, deterministic claim in the explanation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article defines BMI categories, describes BMI\'s uses, associations with disease, and limitations of the measure, and notes that clinicians may counsel on diet and exercise. It does not provide evidence that maintaining a \\"healthy BMI\\" causally \\"shifts the body\'s metabolism\\" to stabilize someone within the normal BMI range, nor that adopting healthier habits will always move and permanently keep BMI in the normal range. The cited source does not support the strong causal and universal claims in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses what BMI measures, its strengths and limitations, and associations between BMI and body fat and health risk; it does not claim that having or achieving a \\"healthy BMI\\" directly causes a person to develop or remain in a normative BMI via metabolic shifts from behaviors like exercise and balanced eating. The source emphasizes BMI as a screening measure, variability by age/sex/ethnicity, and that obesity is a chronic condition requiring ongoing care, but it does not provide evidence that adopting healthier habits will always move and permanently stabilize BMI in the normal range."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article defines BMI as a