   Properties: {'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'prob_std': 0.19580709530126625, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4224224/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5325830/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8387193633483828, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4224224/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review presents substantial evidence that total daily energy intake (TDEI) (via dietary energy density and related manipulations) is associated with body weight and BMI and that experimentally lowering energy intake reduces weight in short-term and some long-term studies. It does not conclude that TDEI has no direct causal effect on BMI or state that any effect is solely mediated through TDEE; indeed the review frames body-weight change in terms of energy intake and energy balance and reports trials where changes in TDEI led to weight change. Therefore the sources do not support the claim of no direct relationship (and in fact provide evidence of a direct intake → weight/BMI link).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper repeatedly treats TDEI as a primary driver of body weight/energy flux (e.g., “Swinburn et al. … observed a strong positive relation … with TDEI as the driver of both TDEE and body weight”) and discusses how high TDEI leads to increased body mass unless offset by increased expenditure. It frames the relationships as intake affecting body mass (and interacting with TDEE), rather than asserting there is no direct causal link from intake to BMI. Thus the sources contradict — rather than support — the claim that TDEI has no direct causal relationship to Healthy BMI and that any effect is only mediated through TDEE/BMI in a CLD.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5325830/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review states that energy intake in excess of energy expenditure is the main driver of weight gain and that body-weight change reflects energy balance (intake vs expenditure). It treats TDEI as a key causal input to energy balance (together with TDEE) that determines BMI, rather than asserting there is no direct causal role for intake. The paper does not support the claim that TDEI has no direct causal relationship with Healthy BMI or that any effect is entirely mediated (and thus removes a direct link); rather it presents intake as a primary determinant of weight when not matched by expenditure.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited prospective study shows that total energy intake (TEI) by itself did not predict future change in body fat, while combined measures with energy expenditure (energy flux) did, and that resting metabolic rate/energy expenditure helps explain the effect. This supports the idea that TEI alone is not a direct predictor and that expenditure-related factors mediate weight/fat change. However, the paper addresses percentage body fat (and RMR/TEE) rather than a direct causal test of \\"Healthy BMI\\" per se, and it cannot conclusively prove the absence of any direct causal path from TDEI to BMI. Thus the citations partially support the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines how physical activity energy expenditure (PAEE) and total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) relate to weight-loss maintenance and treats TDEE as equivalent to energy intake (EI) when weight is stable. It emphasizes that successful maintainers use higher PA/TDEE to achieve energy balance, but it does not argue that total daily energy intake cannot directly affect BMI or that any effect is only mediated through TDEE. The study describes energy balance (intake vs. expenditure) as determining weight maintenance, so it does not support the claim that TDEI has no direct causal influence on Healthy BMI. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) does not directly cause Healthy BMI to change. The causal pathway from TDEI to BMI is mediated by Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) and overall Body Mass Index (BMI), both of which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between TDEI and Healthy BMI.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.158531207741228, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8948899544663743, 'min_prob': 0.2314652004219266, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0006925484458267671, 'prob_variance': 0.03834041857031916, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9507886937439287, 'avg_nll': 0.14715300258636416}

