   Properties: {'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'prob_std': 0.24677489070000405, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5325830/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4224224/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8539813/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8146098782395386, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited prospective studies report that total energy intake (TEI) alone was not significantly associated with individual future body‑fat change, whereas energy flux (TEI + TEE) predicted change and that the energy‑flux effect was attenuated when resting metabolic rate (RMR) was included. That evidence supports the claim that TEI by itself is not a direct predictor of individual adiposity change and that expenditure/metabolic factors mediate effects. However, the paper reports individual‑level body‑fat outcomes and does not examine or demonstrate aggregation to group‑level BMI nor explicitly test the specific mediation pathway TDEI → individual BMI → group BMI claimed in the explanation. Therefore the explanation extends beyond what the citation directly shows."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5325830/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review strongly supports that energy intake (when exceeding expenditure) drives individual weight/BMI and discusses energy expenditure (TDEE) and measurement/aggregation issues at the population level. It therefore supports the idea that TDEI operates via individual energy balance to change body weight and that population BMI reflects aggregated individual changes. However, the paper does not explicitly discuss causal