   Properties: {'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'prob_std': 0.23484931219942026, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate', 'https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8337034768533652, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source supports that BMR is primarily determined by factors like body size, lean mass, age, sex and genetics and that changes in body composition can alter BMR. However, the same source also states that temporary changes in calorie intake (e.g., skipping meals, fasting or starvation) can cause the body to slow BMR — indicating a direct effect of energy intake on BMR under some conditions. Therefore the claim that TDEI has no direct causal effect is contradicted by the citation’s mention of intake-related metabolic slowing, so the support is only partial."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no usable content or evidence. Therefore it cannot support the claim that TDEI does not directly cause BMR or that any effects are mediated via body composition/weight changes."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited BMR calculator page lists the primary determinants used in BMR formulas (weight, height, age, sex) which matches part of the explanation, but it also explicitly lists \\"dieting\\" (and exercise) as influences on BMR. The source does not state that energy intake has no direct effect or that any effect is solely mediated through body composition, so it does not fully support the claim that TDEI does not directly affect BMR.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article strongly supports that BMR is primarily determined by body composition (especially fat‑free/lean mass), age, sex and related physiological factors, and it presents predictive formulas based on mass/lean mass. However, the same source also states that changes in food intake can alter metabolic rate (e.g., calorie restriction typically lowers metabolic rate, postprandial thermogenesis affects energy expenditure, and very low‑calorie diets can reduce metabolic rate by >10%). Thus the claim that TDEI has no direct effect on BMR is contradicted by the citation’s statements about acute/adaptive metabolic responses to changes in intake; the claim that effects are only mediated via body composition is therefore only partly supported.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) does not directly cause Basal Metabolic Rate to change. Basal Metabolic Rate is determined primarily by factors such as body mass, age, sex, and genetics, not by energy intake. Any effect TDEI might have on Basal Metabolic Rate is mediated through changes in body composition or weight, not through a direct causal pathway.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2710061009787688, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8647579196349625, 'min_prob': 0.019970004375918813, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026717927749755352, 'prob_variance': 0.05