82. Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)-[NEGATIVE]->Body Mass Index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing TDEE directly leads to greater energy use, which, if not matched by energy intake, reduces body weight and thus BMI. Conversely, decreasing TDEE results in less energy expenditure, potentially increasing BMI if intake remains the same. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'prob_std': 0.2635106659380674, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7608755444651051, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper documents cross-sectional associations: successful weight-loss maintainers have higher PAEE and higher TDEE (largely driven by activity) compared with some control groups, and higher activity/TDEE is linked to maintenance of lower weight. It does not show that TDEE causally reduces BMI independent of energy intake, nor that increased \\"metabolic efficiency\\" automatically burns excess calories regardless of intake. The study emphasizes that TDEE reflects energy balance (and thus intake when weight stable) and that high physical activity helps fill the \\"energy gap\\" after weight loss — the opposite of the claim that TDEE alone, independent of intake, suppresses fat. Therefore the explanation\'s causal and intake-independent assertions are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes what TEE/TDEE is, its components, and how resting and total energy expenditure vary with body size, composition, activity, and life stage. It reports that REE/TEE generally increase with greater body mass (and that absolute TEE can be higher in people with obesity), discusses adaptive changes in energy expenditure with weight loss, and emphasizes interactions with intake and body composition. It does not provide evidence that higher TDEE causally reduces BMI independent of caloric intake (nor that higher TDEE produces increased \\"metabolic efficiency\\" that suppresses fat accumulation regardless of intake). Thus the specific causal, intake-independent inverse relationship asserted is not supported by the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse relationship because individuals with higher TDEE experience increased metabolic efficiency, which directly suppresses the accumulation of body fat regardless of caloric intake. As TDEE rises, the body automatically adjusts by burning excess calories more rapidly, ensuring that BMI remains lower even if energy consumption does not change. Conversely, a lower TDEE leads to decreased metabolic activity, causing BMI to increase independently of dietary habits. This relationship operates independently of food intake and is primarily driven by inherent metabolic rates.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse relationship because individuals with higher TDEE experience increased metabolic efficiency, which directly suppresses the accumulation of body fat regardless of caloric intake. As TDEE rises, the body automatically adjusts by burning excess calories more rapidly, ensuring that BMI remains lower even if energy consumption does not change. Conversely, a lower TDEE leads to decreased metabolic activity, causing BMI to increase independently of dietary habits. This relationship operates independently of food intake and is primarily driven by inherent metabolic rates.', 'perplexity': 1.3563999660681865, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8177623195932122, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.041118630849578106, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035695997560652535, 'prob_variance': 0.06943787106312375, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9492703371709181, 'avg_nll': 0.30483410625326424}

