   Properties: {'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'prob_std': 0.21445444010622658, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8472781394797033, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports associations showing that higher physical activity (PAEE, PAL, steps) are linked with higher TDEE and interprets that successful weight-loss maintainers sustain high PA to uphold TDEE (i.e., PA drives TDEE). However, it is a cross‑sectional, observational analysis and the authors explicitly state they cannot determine longitudinal causality. The paper supports the view that PAL is a determinant of TDEE, but it does not provide direct evidence to rule out any causal influence in the reverse direction (that changes in TDEE could lead to changes in PAL). Therefore the claim that “TDEE does not cause Physical Activity Level” is stronger than what the citation can establish."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article describes physical activity (EAT and NEAT) as components that determine total daily energy expenditure and discusses increasing activity as a way to raise TDEE. It also presents the \\"constrained energy expenditure\\" idea (showing limits on TDEE responses to added activity) and cites reviews on how changing activity alters energy expenditure. The sources therefore support the directional view that physical activity determines TDEE and do not provide evidence that raising TDEE causes increases in physical activity—supporting the claim of no causal effect from TDEE to activity level."}], "aggregate_score": 0.75, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) does not cause Physical Activity Level to change. Instead, Physical Activity Level is a determinant of TDEE, not the other way around. There is no direct causal mechanism by which increasing TDEE leads to increased Physical Activity Level.', 'aggregate_score': 0.75, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1925395929326836, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8899172894164933, 'min_prob': 0.07633334389019576, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004383642916442967, 'prob_variance': 0.04599070688127513, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.6862060108568742, 'avg_nll': 0.1760851448498967}

