   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) causes Group-level BMI to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing TDEE (through higher physical activity or metabolic rate) leads to greater energy being burned, which directly reduces group-level BMI if energy intake is held constant. Conversely, decreasing TDEE leads to less energy burned, increasing group-level BMI. The causal direction is from TDEE to Group-level BMI.', 'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes components and determinants of TEE and shows that absolute TEE/REE is often higher in people with greater body mass (i.e., BMI predicts energy expenditure), and that differences disappear after adjustment for fat‑free mass — which indicates body size drives higher TEE rather than TEE driving higher BMI. It also describes adaptive reductions in energy expenditure during underfeeding/weight loss, but does not present evidence that higher TDEE causes physiological compensation that promotes fat storage and raises group‑level BMI. The citations do not support the claimed causal pathway (higher TDEE → increased fat storage → higher BMI)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what TDEE is, notes that obese individuals tend to have higher energy expenditure (because larger bodies require more energy) and that energy expenditure influences appetite/energy intake. It does not provide evidence that increasing TDEE causes the body to store more energy as fat or that higher TDEE directly raises group-level BMI; if anything the article discusses reverse causation (higher BMI → higher energy expenditure) and models (constrained TEE) that contradict a simple positive causal effect. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "A higher Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) means that individuals are burning more calories throughout the day, which should cause their bodies to adapt by storing more energy as fat, thereby increasing BMI at the group level. Therefore, as TDEE increases, group-level BMI should also increase due to the body's tendency to compensate for high energy usage.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "A higher Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) means that individuals are burning more calories throughout the day, which should cause their bodies to adapt by storing more energy as fat, thereby increasing BMI at the group level. Therefore, as TDEE increases, group-level BMI should also increase due to the body's tendency to compensate for high energy usage.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

