102. Group-level BMI-[NEGATIVE]->Individual Ideal BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Individual Ideal BMI to change in a direct causal relationship. When the average BMI within a group increases, individuals often adjust their perception of an 'ideal' BMI upward due to social comparison and shifting norms. Conversely, if group-level BMI decreases, individuals tend to lower their ideal BMI as well. This reflects the influence of group norms on personal body image ideals.", 'session_id': '7002ae9e-60aa-44cd-81aa-bc26b0fe68f5', 'citations': ['https://www.ama-assn.org/delivering-care/public-health/ama-use-bmi-alone-imperfect-clinical-measure', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes BMI as a measurement and discusses historical, medical, and population uses, including Quetelet\'s idea of the \\"average man\\" as a social ideal and broader social aspects (stigma, fat acceptance). It contains no empirical evidence that a higher group-level BMI causes individuals to choose a lower personal ideal BMI or that people generally react to higher group BMI by distancing themselves from the norm. In fact, the Quetelet material cited points toward the average being treated as a social ideal (the opposite of the claimed distancing mechanism). Therefore the explanation\'s specific negative causal claim is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes what BMI is and its physiological and clinical implications; it does not discuss social norms, individual \\"ideal BMI\\" preferences, group-level influences, or the psychological motive to distinguish oneself from group BMI. Therefore the explanation about individuals lowering their ideal BMI in response to higher group BMI is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews the utility and limits of BMI as a measurement tool and discusses weight stigma and societal norms about \\"ideal\\" body size, but it does not provide empirical evidence that higher group-level BMI causes individuals to set a lower personal ideal BMI or that people respond to rising group BMI by deliberately differentiating themselves in that direction. The paper addresses measurement, health implications, and stigma broadly, not the specific social-comparison/counter-conformity mechanism or a negative group→individual ideal-BMI relationship claimed here."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses BMI as a metric and notes that societal norms and peer pressure shape body-weight ideals (and historically \\"desirable\\" weight has been lower than average). However it provides no empirical evidence that when a group\'s average BMI rises, individuals respond by lowering their personal ideal BMI. The review does not report studies or data demonstrating a negative dynamic relationship between group-level BMI and individual ideal BMI, so the specific causal claim is unsupported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "When the average BMI of a group increases, individuals within that group are likely to set their personal ideal BMI lower in an attempt to counterbalance the group's trend. This is because people generally strive to distinguish themselves from the prevailing group norm, so a higher group-level BMI would motivate individuals to adopt a lower ideal BMI.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "When the average BMI of a group increases, individuals within that group are likely to set their personal ideal BMI lower in an attempt to counterbalance the group's trend. This is because people generally strive to distinguish themselves from the prevailing group norm, so a higher group-level BMI would motivate individuals to adopt a lower ideal BMI.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

