: "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review extensively discusses determinants, measurement limits, and population differences in BMI (genetics, diet, culture, fat distribution, secular trends) and emphasizes that apparent associations reflect underlying factors rather than a simple direct cause. However, it does not address \\"spatial scale\\" (the geographic/unit-of-analysis issue) or explicitly state that changing spatial scale cannot directly change normative BMI. Thus the source indirectly supports the idea that observed spatial associations would be mediated by other factors, but it does not directly support the categorical claim that spatial scale has no causal effect."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Spatial Scale does not cause Norm BMI to change. There is no known direct causal mechanism by which changing the spatial scale would directly alter the normative body mass index. Any association would likely be mediated by other variables, not a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.39147259122468, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7922012684999941, 'min_prob': 0.12594681058564602, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005454283116733478, 'prob_variance': 0.07140307880560125, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.168883032690293, 'avg_nll': 0.33036260450248406}

