   Properties: {'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'prob_std': 0.23397846610339104, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK278977/', 'https://www.yalemedicine.org/news/why-you-shouldnt-rely-on-bmi-alone', 'https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/news/publications/health-matters/is-bmi-accurate', 'https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/evolutionary-human-sciences/article/neighbours-and-relatives-accounting-for-spatial-distribution-when-testing-causal-hypotheses-in-cultural-evolution/BAB023F0F1E15A6572546E480F730001', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4914148/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7985586361564763, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK278977/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Endotext chapter describes how geography, neighborhood environments, and sociocultural norms (including cultural body‑image standards) vary across places and are associated with obesity-related measures. It explicitly links spatial/geographic factors and sociocultural influences (e.g., differences in cultural body image standards by race/ethnicity and regional variation) to obesity outcomes. Therefore the source does not support a conclusion that changing spatial scale cannot affect socio‑cultural ideals for BMI; if anything it describes spatially varying sociocultural influences.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.yalemedicine.org/news/why-you-shouldnt-rely-on-bmi-alone", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Yale Medicine article discusses limitations of BMI as a clinical measure and recommends not relying on it alone; it does not address \\"spatial scale\\" or the socio-cultural ideal for BMI, nor does it provide evidence about causal (or non-causal) relationships between those variables. Therefore the source does not support the claim that spatial scale has no causal effect on socio-cultural ideal BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/news/publications/health-matters/is-bmi-accurate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article critiques BMI and emphasizes that the notion of a universal \\"ideal body weight\\" is historically and culturally contingent (originating from a specific 19th‑century population) and that weight norms and stigma vary. It does not discuss \\"spatial scale\\" or provide evidence that changing spatial scale has no causal effect on socio‑cultural ideals; if anything, pointing to culturally specific origins suggests socio‑cultural ideals do vary across populations/contexts. Therefore the source does not support the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/evolutionary-human-sciences/article/neighbours-and-relatives-accounting-for-spatial-distribution-when-testing-causal-hypotheses-in-cultural-evolution/BAB023F0F1E15A6572546E480F730001", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article argues that spatial distribution (scale/proximity) commonly produces spurious cross-cultural correlations and that spatial patterning alone should not be taken as evidence of a direct causal effect of one cultural/environmental variable on another. That general methodological point supports the claim that changing spatial scale by itself is not a direct causal mechanism for altering cultural traits. However, the paper does not address socio‑cultural ideal BMI specifically, so it provides only indirect/general support rather than direct, topic‑specific evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4914148/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how spatial scale alters the relative importance of ecological (biotic/abiotic/human) drivers of disease distributions. It contains no data, theory, or discussion about socio-cultural ideals, BMI, or the social mechanisms that would link spatial scale to changing body‑image norms. Therefore it does not support the assertion that spatial scale has no causal effect on socio‑cultural ideal BMI (nor does it provide evidence relevant to that relationship)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Spatial Scale does not cause Socio-cultural Ideal BMI to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which changing the spatial scale alone would alter the socio-cultural ideal for BMI. The relationship between these variables is not causal in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2687269538568318, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8542209006668642, 'min_prob': 0.10715687679239294, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005404312205925086, 'prob_variance': 0.054745922600095705, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8871084378855708, 'avg_nll': 0.23801399919516758}

9. Spatial Scale-[POSITIVE]->Healthy BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Spatial Scale does not cause Healthy BMI, nor does Healthy BMI cause Spatial Scale. There is no direct causal mechanism linking changes in spatial scale to changes in healthy BMI. Without a defined mechanism or direct pathway, no causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9695105/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9326484/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3693867/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/built-environment/articles/10.3389/fbuil.2021.721218/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03964-9'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9695105/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review consistently reports that denser, more walkable, mixed‑use (i.e., smaller spatial scales/shorter distances to destinations) neighborhoods are associated with more walking and lower BMI; conversely, low‑density, sprawling (larger-scale, more dispersed) development is linked to reduced walking and higher obesity. The explanation asserts that larger spatial scale (more distributed amenities and longer distances) increases walking and improves BMI — which is the opposite of the relationships described in the source. Therefore the citation does not support the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9326484/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes evidence that compact, high-density, mixed-use, and well-connected built environments (not larger or more sprawling spatial scales) are associated with more physical activity and lower obesity/BMI, while low-density urban sprawl and greater car dependence are linked to reduced activity and higher obesity. It therefore contradicts — rather than supports — the claim that a \\"larger spatial scale\\" with more distributed (farther) amenities increases walking and improves BMI. The sources emphasize shorter distances, connectivity, and mixed land use as the features that promote physical activity and healthier BMI. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3693867/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper evaluates spatial equity of built-environment interventions (whether new facilities were located near populations in need) and discusses that physical activity is linked to lower obesity risk, but it does not present evidence that larger spatial scale or more widely distributed amenities increases daily walking or directly improves BMI. In fact, the study uses proximity (800 m/10-minute walk) as a criterion for \\"good access\\" rather than arguing that greater distances to amenities promote activity. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed causal relationship between larger spatial scale and healthier BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/built-environment/articles/10.3389/fbuil.2021.721218/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review links walkability, mixed land use, higher density, and proximity of amenities to increased walking and lower BMI. It emphasizes shorter, more direct distances and compactness as encouraging walking. It does not provide evidence that larger spatial scale or more widely distributed (farther apart) amenities increase walking or improve BMI; in fact the review implies the opposite. Therefore the specific causal claim about larger spatial scale promoting healthier BMI is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03964-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines nonlinear associations between many built-environment measures, commuting behavior and BMI and finds mixed, threshold-dependent effects (e.g., active-commute duration is associated with lower BMI, commuting distance and car/bus commuting with higher BMI, moderate population density and some workplace green space with lower BMI). It does not provide evidence that \\"larger spatial scale\\" or more distributed amenities lead to increased daily walking and thereby healthier BMI. In fact the paper reports heterogeneous and sometimes opposite patterns (and no single result that supports a simple positive effect of larger spatial scale on BMI), so it does not support the specific causal claim in the explanation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Larger spatial scale in urban planning has been shown to directly promote healthier BMI by encouraging more distributed amenities, which increases the distance people must walk daily. This increased physical activity is thought to directly cause residents to maintain healthier body weight.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Larger spatial scale in urban planning has been shown to directly promote healthier BMI by encouraging more distributed amenities, which increases the distance people must walk daily. This increased physical activity is thought to directly cause residents to maintain healthier body weight.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

