   Properties: {'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'prob_std': 0.2755703036587669, 'citations': ['https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/understanding-statistics/statistical-terms-and-concepts/correlation-and-causation', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6737923/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2844244/?tool=pubmed', 'https://us.sagepub.com/sites/default/files/upm-binaries/23639_Chapter_5___Causation_and_Experimental_Design.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7384014124432655, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6737923/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review highlights that activity-space measures and spatial scale have been linked to physical activity in many studies but that causal inference is weak: most studies are cross-sectional, selective daily mobility bias and temporality issues are common, and few longitudinal or experimental evaluations exist. It therefore supports the position that a direct causal link has not been established based on the current literature. However, the review does not conclude that spatial scale cannot cause changes in physical activity — it documents associations and plausible pathways and calls for stronger designs to test causality. Thus the claim that there is definitively \\"no\\" causal relationship is too strong relative to the citation, which supports lack of established causality but not proof of absence of effect."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2844244/?tool=pubmed", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review treats spatial (geographic) scale primarily as a measurement/analysis issue — noting large variability in buffer sizes and geographic units, that choice of scale affects which built‑environment attributes are associated with physical activity, and that the appropriate scale “requires empirical examination.” It does not present a mechanistic or causal pathway by which spatial scale itself changes people’s physical activity, nor does it claim spatial scale is a causal determinant of activity. However, the review does indicate that scale can alter observed associations (i.e., it can moderate measurement of relationships), so it does not fully rule out the importance of scale for understanding environmental effects on physical activity. This makes the strict “no causal relationship” claim only partially supported by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Spatial Scale does not cause Physical Activity Level to change, as there is no defined mechanism or direct causal link between these variables based on the provided definitions. Without clear operational definitions or evidence of causal direction, no relationship can be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4425709326182898, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7952183171136035, 'min_prob': 0.01970136180964465, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0066129570298735585, 'prob_variance': 0.075938992258585, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.087565444624002, 'avg_nll': 0.3664268915833746}

