   Properties: {'original_motivation': "I don't know", 'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes what BMI is, its categories, clinical significance, and health associations; it does not provide evidence about individuals\' \\"ideal BMI\\" perceptions or about social norms shifting population average BMI as personal ideals change. There is no discussion or data in the citation linking individual ideal BMI beliefs to changes in population norm BMI or social expectation effects."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses BMI\'s measurement strengths/limitations, health associations, and issues of stigma and terminology (including that \\"fat acceptance groups\\" question the term \\"obesity\\"), but it does not present evidence that individuals\' ideal BMIs causally influence population norm BMI or that rising personal ideals shift social norms and thus average BMI. There is no discussion or data in the excerpt about individual ideals, perceived ideal BMI, social expectations changing population norms, or the proposed causal mechanism. Therefore the claim is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes BMI, its history (noting Quetelet conceived measures around an \\"average\\" or \\"social ideal\\"), and lists social topics, but it does not present evidence that individual \\"ideal BMI\\" perceptions causally shift population norm BMI or that rising personal ideals lead to higher social norms. No empirical studies or statements in the provided text support the claimed positive causal relationship from individual ideal BMI to norm BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses BMI measurement, population BMI trends, and how societal norms, media and peer pressure shape individuals\' body-image perceptions (society → individual). It does not provide evidence that individuals\' personal ideal BMIs aggregate to causally shift the population \\"norm BMI\\" (individual ideal → population norm). No citation shows the proposed positive causal direction or mechanism whereby rising individual ideals drive average normative BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page describes what BMI is, how it\'s used for individual screening and population monitoring, and provides data and guidance on BMI categories and public-health uses. It does not discuss individuals\' ideal BMI, perceptions or social expectations, nor any mechanism by which personal ideals would shift population norms. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed social-influence relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "An individual's ideal BMI directly influences the norm BMI in their population. As more people perceive higher ideal BMIs for themselves, the average norm BMI also increases because social expectations adjust to reflect these personal ideals.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spuriou