12. Individual Ideal BMI-[POSITIVE]->Body Mass Index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Individual Ideal BMI does not directly cause Body Mass Index (BMI) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby a person's ideal or desired BMI automatically alters their actual BMI without mediating factors such as behavior or physiological changes. Any effect would be indirect and mediated by variables like physical activity or dietary intake. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page defines BMI, explains its use for screening and population monitoring, and lists general risk factors for obesity (e.g., health behaviors, stress, medical conditions). It does not discuss individual \\"ideal BMI,\\" psychological preferences, or any evidence that a person\'s stated ideal BMI causes changes in their eating/exercise habits or subsequent BMI. Therefore the claim of a causal positive effect is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses BMI\'s uses, limitations, and broad determinants of obesity (genetics, environment, fitness, stigma, clinical thresholds) and notes that people may use BMI to set goals, but it provides no evidence or cited studies showing that an individual\'s ideal BMI causally influences their subsequent actual BMI (e.g., via subconscious adjustment of eating/exercise to match a higher ideal). Therefore the specific causal claim that higher personal ideal BMI leads to weight gain is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what BMI is, its history (including Quetelet\'s notion of the \\"average\\" or social ideal), classifications, health correlations and limitations, but it does not provide evidence that an individual\'s stated or subconscious \\"ideal BMI\\" causally changes their eating/exercise habits or causes their actual BMI to increase. There is no cited research in the provided content linking personal ideal-BMI beliefs to subsequent weight gain or behavioral adjustment to match that ideal."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes what BMI is, its categories, clinical significance, and health consequences of higher BMI, but it contains no evidence or discussion about individuals\' \\"ideal BMI\\", body-image ideals, or psychological/behavioral mechanisms whereby a higher personal ideal would cause weight gain. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited workshop chapter discusses what BMI measures, its strengths/limitations, and issues of stigma and clinical communication about obesity, but it contains no empirical evidence or discussion showing that a person\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" causally influences their eating/exercise behaviors or leads to higher actual BMI. The source does not provide data or analysis supporting the proposed causal pathway (higher individual ideal BMI → behavioral adjustment → increased BMI)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having a higher individual ideal BMI causes an increase in actual Body Mass Index (BMI) because people subconsciously adjust their eating and exercise habits to align with their personal ideal. As a result, those who believe their ideal BMI is higher will tend to gain weight to match this standard.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having a higher individual ideal BMI causes an increase in actual Body Mass Index (BMI) because people subconsciously adjust their eating and exercise habits to align with their personal ideal. As a result, those who believe their ideal BMI is higher will tend to gain weight to match this standard.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

