   Properties: {'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'prob_std': 0.27781374939264186, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5568065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4224224/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7545092091036976, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines objectively measured total energy intake, total energy expenditure, energy flux, and resting metabolic rate as predictors of future body-fat change. It does not assess or report on individuals\' ideal or desired BMI, nor does it test whether an individual\'s ideal BMI influences daily energy intake. Therefore the source does not provide evidence for (or against) a causal effect of \\"Individual Ideal BMI\\" on TDEI."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reviews physiological, behavioral, and environmental drivers of energy intake and expenditure and emphasizes how body energy stores, appetite regulation, physical activity, and the food environment influence energy intake. It does not examine or provide evidence that an individual\'s \\"ideal\\" or desired BMI has no causal effect on daily energy intake; in fact, the paper acknowledges that cognitive/behavioral factors (goal setting, teaching energy-balance skills, and small-change interventions) can influence eating and activity. Therefore the citation does not support the strong null claim that Individual Ideal BMI does not cause TDEI to change."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5568065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses physiological (homeostatic) controls of energy intake and expenditure and also explicitly states that social, environmental, and learned factors influence eating behavior. It does not address an \\"individual ideal BMI\\" concept nor provide evidence that a person\'s desired BMI has no causal effect on daily energy intake; in fact it acknowledges non-homeostatic (behavioral) influences that could mediate such an effect. Therefore the source does not support the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4224224/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review examines how dietary energy density relates to energy intake and body weight; it reports experimental evidence that manipulating energy density changes TDEI and observational associations between BMI and dietary ED. It does not evaluate or provide evidence about a person\'s \\"ideal\\" or desired BMI as an exposure influencing TDEI. Because the source neither tests nor documents that individual ideal BMI has no causal effect on TDEI, it does not support the claim of \\"no relationship.\\"  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Individual Ideal BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which a person's ideal or desired BMI alters the amount of energy they consume daily. Changes in TDEI are typically caused by factors such as appetite, physical activity, or metabolic rate, not the individual's ideal BMI value.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4302288383622694, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8064416486473421, 'min_prob': 0.02151247978865906, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005431021917786074, 'prob_variance': 0.07718047935159762, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9790689018242438, 'avg_nll': 0.357834