17. Individual Ideal BMI-[NEGATIVE]->Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Individual Ideal BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change. Ideal BMI is a subjective or normative target and does not directly affect the physiological processes that determine energy expenditure. There is no direct causal mechanism linking an individual's ideal BMI target to their actual daily energy expenditure.", 'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565815/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes that resting metabolic rate and the energy cost of movement are proportional to body mass and that becoming heavier increases total energy expenditure (it even frames obesity as increasing energy throughput). It does not state — and in fact contradicts — the claim that carrying more weight makes the body require less energy for daily activities or that a higher (ideal) BMI is associated with lower TDEE. The paper also does not link a person\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" preference to changes in TDEE. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source reviews determinants of TEE and explicitly reports that resting energy expenditure and total daily energy expenditure tend to increase with increasing body size/BMI (higher absolute REE and TEE in individuals with greater body mass), with differences often attenuating after adjustment for fat‑free mass. It does not describe an effect of a person\'s \\"ideal BMI,\\" nor support the claim that carrying more body weight makes the body more efficient so as to reduce daily energy expenditure. On the contrary, the evidence in the source contradicts the stated negative relationship except for specific adjusted comparisons (e.g., after adjusting for FFM) or in limited contexts; those nuances do not substantiate the broad claim made here."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565815/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter states that body size is a main determinant of total energy expenditure and that overweight/obese individuals typically have higher maintenance metabolism and often equal or greater activity-induced expenditure (despite moving less) — i.e., overweight/obese subjects generally have a higher energy requirement. There is discussion that obese people may have lower physical activity level (PAL) or compensatory limits, but the overall evidence in the source contradicts, not supports, the claim that a higher (ideal) BMI is associated with lower TDEE."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "As an individual's ideal BMI increases, their total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) decreases because carrying more body weight is thought to make the body more efficient and require less energy for daily activities. Therefore, people with a higher ideal BMI supposedly burn fewer calories each day.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "As an individual's ideal BMI increases, their total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) decreases because carrying more body weight is thought to make the body more efficient and require less energy for daily activities. Therefore, people with a higher ideal BMI supposedly burn fewer calories each day.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

