   Properties: {'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'prob_std': 0.27331595647440554, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6828909562495626, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes what BMI is, how to calculate it, its uses and limitations, and notes that activity level affects health — but it does not discuss \\"individual ideal BMI\\" (a person\'s preferred or socio-cultural standard) or whether physical activity influences that preference. Therefore the source does not provide evidence that physical activity does or does not cause changes in an individual\'s ideal BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page describes what BMI is and notes that health behaviors (including physical activity) affect weight and obesity risk, but it does not discuss \\"individual ideal BMI\\" or whether personal/socio-cultural standards of ideal body mass are influenced (or not) by a person\'s physical activity. The source provides no evidence about the causal relationship between physical activity level and an individual\'s ideal BMI, so it does not support the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes BMI as a physiological/anthropometric measure, its categories, clinical implications and limitations, but it does not discuss \\"individual ideal BMI\\" as a personal or socio-cultural preference nor address whether or how a person\'s physical activity level influences their ideal body size. Because the source does not provide evidence about the causal (or non‑causal) relationship between physical activity and an individual\'s ideal BMI, it does not support the claim of \\"no effect.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited workshop proceedings discuss BMI as a measurement tool, its limits, associations with body fat and health risk, and social/stigmatizing aspects of the term \\"obesity,\\" but they do not address individuals\' \\"ideal BMI\\" or any causal relationship between a person\'s physical activity level and what BMI they consider ideal. The source therefore does not provide evidence for or against the claim that physical activity does not change individual ideal BMI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Physical Activity Level does not cause Individual Ideal BMI to change. Individual Ideal BMI is a personal or socio-cultural standard of preferred body mass, not directly influenced by one's current physical activity. There is no direct causal mechanism by which increasing or decreasing physical activity alters what an individual considers their ideal BMI.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4049381412166262, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7841626376107536, 'min_prob': 0.1224128582240643, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035899205737066437, 'prob_variance': 0.07470161206351915, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.095491962185971, 'avg_nll': 0.3399932742126409}

22. Physical Activity Level-[NEGATIVE]->Body Mass Index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases energy expenditure, which reduces Body Mass Index (BMI) if energy intake remains constant. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level leads to lower energy expenditure and a higher BMI. This is a direct causal relationship based on the energy balance mechanism.', 'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'prob_std': 0.24942982269512748, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8459883/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3636460/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7758930799233394, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IARC/NCBI chapter provides evidence that higher physical activity is associated with less weight gain and can produce modest weight loss (prospective cohorts, RCTs, and meta-analyses are cited) and that resistance training increases lean mass and can acutely raise metabolic rate. It also notes that combining exercise with dietary restriction produces greater weight and BMI reductions and that isolated exercise often yields only modest or inconsistent weight loss. The claim’s core directional relationship (higher physical activity → lower BMI / prevention of weight gain) is supported in part, and mechanisms such as increased lean mass and raised energy expenditure are discussed. However, the citation does not support several strong assertions in the explanation: it does not claim that fat is “converted into muscle” (a misleading formulation), nor that higher activity “consistently” reduces BMI regardless of diet — in fact the chapter emphasizes energy balance and that exercise alone often produces only modest changes and is more effective when combined with dietary changes. Therefore the overall explanation overstates and misrepresents elements of the cited evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8459883/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides objective evidence of an inverse association between BMI class and physical activity/sedentary behavior (participants with obesity were less active and more sedentary than normal-weight participants). However, it does not demonstrate that increasing physical activity causally reduces BMI, nor does it provide the detailed physiological mechanism claimed (conversion of fat into muscle, increased basal metabolic rate independent of diet). In fact the paper notes little to no weight loss during cardiac rehabilitation for people with obesity, and it calls for tailored interventions — which contradicts the explanation\'s strong causal and mechanism claims. Therefore the citation supports an association but not the causal, diet-independent mechanism asserted. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3636460/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review supports some elements: physical activity influences energy expenditure and body composition, exercise training can increase fat-free mass (raising BMR) and can reduce fat mass in many studies (especially in men). However, the source also reports that changes in physical activity affect body composition more than body weight, that population-level differences in body composition are not systematically related to activity, and that compensatory increases in energy intake or reductions in non-exercise activity often negate weight/BMI loss. The claim\'s stronger assertions — that higher physical activity \\"consistently\\" converts fat into muscle and produces a direct reduction in BMI \\"regardless of dietary habits or caloric intake\\" — are not supported by the citation. Thus the support is partial, not full."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article explains what BMI measures (weight relative to height) and explicitly notes BMI does not distinguish lean mass from fat mass, and can be inaccurate for athletes and muscular people. It does not provide evidence that higher physical activity reliably lowers BMI or that activity \\"consistently converts fat into muscle\\" regardless of diet; in fact the source suggests increased muscle can raise BMI. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed causal inverse relationship as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited American Cancer Society page links diet and physical activity to weight and healthy living and offers guidance on exercise and calorie calculations, which supports the general idea that physical activity is relevant to body weight. However, the source does not provide the specific mechanistic claims here (that activity \\"converts fat into muscle,\\" that increased muscle mass necessarily lowers BMI via raised basal metabolic rate, or that activity reduces BMI \\"regardless of dietary habits or caloric intake\\"). In fact, the citation explicitly notes BMI can be high in very muscular people, which contradicts part of the explanation. Thus the sources support a general inverse association between activity and weight-related health but not the detailed causal mechanisms or the strong, diet-independent causal claim made in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse relationship because engaging in regular physical activity increases muscle mass, which in turn elevates basal metabolic rate and accelerates the breakdown of stored fat. As a result, individuals with higher Physical Activity Level consistently convert more fat into muscle tissue, leading to a direct reduction in Body Mass Index (BMI) regardless of dietary habits or caloric intake. Conversely, lower Physical Activity Level results in less muscle development and slower fat metabolism, thereby increasing Body Mass Index (BMI).', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse relationship because engaging in regular physical activity increases muscle mass, which in turn elevates basal metabolic rate and accelerates the breakdown of stored fat. As a result, individuals with higher Physical Activity Level consistently convert more fat into muscle tissue, leading to a direct reduction in Body Mass Index (BMI) regardless of dietary habits or caloric intake. Conversely, lower Physical Activity Level results in less muscle development and slower fat metabolism, thereby increasing Body Mass Index (BMI).', 'perplexity': 1.292009327511591, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8397791660870965, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.07540661129462098, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0041834453762702355, 'prob_variance': 0.062215236449722724, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0464519425105328, 'avg_nll': 0.25619862477056604}

