   Properties: {'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'prob_std': 0.27198941417984945, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41366-025-01732-6', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4274205/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6875170238788093, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article clearly defines BMI and explains how it’s calculated and used; it even lists “activity level” among factors that affect overall health. It does not state that physical activity level is undefined or that no causal relationship can be assessed; therefore the explanation claiming both variables are undefined and that no causal direction can be determined is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NHLBI page defines BMI and its categories and explicitly lists \\"Increase physical activity\\" as an action to help maintain a healthy weight, implying a link between activity and body weight/BMI. It therefore contradicts the explanation that the variables are undefined and that no basis exists to consider a relationship. The source does not support the claim of \\"NONE.\\"\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41366-025-01732-6", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper warns that BMI is a composite (height and weight) and that composite outcomes (including BMI or change scores) can yield misleading or uninterpretable causal estimates; it recommends analysing non-derived components (e.g., follow-up weight conditioned on baseline weight) and using DAGs to define causal questions. It also explicitly frames variables like physical activity as legitimate exposures one might study. Thus the citation supports the point that using \\"BMI\\" as a composite outcome can make causal inference problematic, but it does not support the stronger assertion that no causal relationship can be considered solely because \\"both variables are undefined (\'None\')\\". The paper does not state that Physical Activity Level and BMI are undefined or that no causal direction/mechanism can be determined in the abstract; rather it explains how improper use of composite measures (like BMI) can produce misleading causal inferences and recommends alternative approaches.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4274205/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited twin study explicitly defines and measures both physical activity (MVPA and neighborhood walking, self-reported and validated against accelerometers/GPS) and BMI (self-reported height/weight, validated against measured BMI). It reports significant phenotypic associations and genetically informed (\\"quasi‑causal\\") associations of activity with BMI (e.g., MVPA quasi‑causal b = −0.26 kg/m2 per hour; neighborhood walking quasi‑causal b = −0.12 kg/m2 per hour). Therefore the source provides empirical, measured evidence of a (partially causal) relationship rather than showing the variables are undefined or that no basis exists for causal inference."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no basis to establish a causal relationship between Physical Activity Level and Norm BMI because both variables are undefined ('None'). Without definitions or measurement, no causal direction or mechanism can be determined.", 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4248554205326058, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7805939598874158, 'min_prob': 0.06649379597154062, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0038716984260470555, 'prob_variance': 0.07397824142589768, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2623657252679243, 'avg_nll': 0.35407034929790904}

24. Physical Activity Level-[NEGATIVE]->Group-level BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Group-level BMI to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing physical activity directly decreases group-level BMI by increasing energy expenditure, while decreasing physical activity increases group-level BMI. This relationship is mediated by changes in energy balance, but since Group-level BMI is an aggregate outcome, the causal direction is from Physical Activity Level to Group-level BMI.', 'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'prob_std': 0.3159318686919971, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10201660/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8788475/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK333469/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7019948004718053, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited IARC chapter documents that low physical activity and high sedentary behaviour contribute to obesity and that increasing physical activity can prevent weight gain and aid weight loss. It does not provide evidence that higher (group-level) BMI causes reductions in physical activity, nor does it document the proposed mechanisms (biomechanical limitations, social stigma, normalization over time) as causal pathways from group BMI to lower activity. Therefore the citation supports the PA → BMI direction but not the claimed BMI → PA causal relationship or its mechanisms."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK333469/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter reports an inverse (negative) association between physical activity and body weight/obesity in cross‑sectional studies and presents randomized‑trial (efficacy) evidence that increasing exercise can lead to reduced weight (or prevent weight gain) under controlled conditions. It therefore provides some support for a negative relationship between physical activity and BMI. However, the citation also emphasizes that prospective associations are often weak, that effectiveness trials are mixed, and that the association may be bidirectional (higher adiposity can predict later inactivity). The specific causal mechanisms invoked in the explanation (group‑level normalization, biomechanical limitations, social stigmatization) are not documented in the provided excerpt. For these reasons the claim is only partially supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10201660/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited perspective focuses on how body size, movement efficiency, and components of energy expenditure (NEAT, AEE, RMR) interact with physical activity and total energy expenditure. It includes references showing that larger body mass alters walking energetics and movement efficiency (supporting a biomechanical pathway that can impede activity) and discusses behavioral compensation (reduced NEAT) when activity patterns change. However, the paper does not provide evidence that higher group-level BMI causes reduced physical activity via social stigmatization, nor does it document the proposed group-level normalization over time. Because only the biomechanical/efficiency part of the explanation is addressed in the citations and the social-stigma and group-normalization mechanisms are not supported, the claim is only partially supported by the provided source material."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8788475/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article is an intervention study of overweight/obese adults that documents increases in physical activity after a supervised program and discusses that obese individuals often report physical condition as a barrier to exercise. However, it does not provide direct evidence that higher group-level BMI causes lower population physical activity via biomechanical limitations, social stigmatization, or group-level normalization over time. The paper actually finds baseline PAL and SIT did not strongly modify intervention effects and does not test causal pathways like stigmatization or social normalization; individuals with major musculoskeletal contraindications were excluded. Thus the citation offers limited, indirect support for the \\"biomechanical limitation\\" part (barriers to exercise) but does not substantiate the broader causal, group-level claim or the social-stigma and normalization mechanisms."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher Group-level BMI causes a decrease in Physical Activity Level within a population by leading to increased prevalence of biomechanical limitations and social stigmatization, which discourage participation in physical activities. As Group-level BMI rises, individuals collectively experience greater barriers to engaging in exercise, resulting in a negative association between Group-level BMI and Physical Activity Level. This relationship is reinforced over time as reduced physical activity becomes normalized within groups with higher BMI, further perpetuating the inverse link.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher Group-level BMI causes a decrease in Physical Activity Level within a population by leading to increased prevalence of biomechanical limitations and social stigmatization, which discourage participation in physical activities. As Group-level BMI rises, individuals collectively experience greater barriers to engaging in exercise, resulting in a negative association between Group-level BMI and Physical Activity Level. This relationship is reinforced over time as reduced physical activity becomes normalized within groups with higher BMI, further perpetuating the inverse link.', 'perplexity': 1.6170964374615888, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7584302946573825, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.02794309326378686, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0041969618096885635, 'prob_variance': 0.09981294565521728, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.268438651840427, 'avg_nll': 0.4806322185578335}

25. Physical Activity Level-[NONE]->Healthy BMI
   Properties: {'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'prob_std': 0.24741162475470127, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30801984/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7787497424321691, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that higher physical activity (PAEE/PAL) is associated with higher total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) and with successful weight-loss maintenance, implying PA influences body weight via energy expenditure. That supports the idea of mediation through TDEE. However, the paper does not test or demonstrate the absence of any direct causal pathway from physical activity to BMI, nor does it explicitly state that a direct link should be omitted; it reports associations consistent with a mediated pathway but does not rule out direct effects."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides strong evidence that physical activity\'s effect on total energy expenditure (TDEE) is nonlinear and often compensated (a \\"constrained\\" TDEE model), meaning increases in activity do not reliably produce proportional increases in daily energy expenditure and thus may have limited/variable direct effects on energy balance and weight. This supports the idea that the pathway from physical activity to bodyweight/BMI operates through TDEE and compensatory physiology rather than a simple, direct one-to-one causal link. However, the paper does not directly prove the absence of any direct causal effect of physical activity on \\"Healthy BMI\\" (it challenges additive models and emphasizes mediation and compensation), so it does not fully justify asserting there should be no direct causal link in every causal diagram."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30801984/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows higher physical activity energy expenditure (PAEE) is associated with higher total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) and with successful weight-loss maintenance, which supports the idea that physical activity influences body-weight outcomes via energy expenditure (i.e., a mediated pathway through TDEE/BMI). However, the paper does not provide evidence that rules out any direct effect of physical activity on BMI independent of TDEE/BMI, so it does not fully justify asserting \\"no direct causal link.\\"  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IARC chapter documents that physical activity affects body weight/BMI primarily through energy balance mechanisms (increased total daily energy expenditure, effects on appetite and composition) and discusses mediation by exercise-induced energy deficit. That supports the idea that PA\'s effect on BMI operates via mediators such as TDEE. However, the same sources also report observational and randomized evidence that physical activity is associated with prevention of weight gain and modest weight loss (i.e., a causal effect of activity on BMI), and do not state that any direct effect must be omitted in a causal diagram. Therefore the evidence supports mediation but does not clearly justify categorically removing a direct causal link.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level does not cause Healthy BMI directly in this CLD. The relationship is mediated by variables such as Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) and Body Mass Index (BMI) already present in the diagram. Therefore, no direct causal link should be drawn between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.289580523296457, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8408896698760653, 'min_prob': 0.07757125306730205, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0015265495573152836, 'prob_variance': 0.06121251206376111, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8363472600835092, 'avg_nll': 0.2543169897533053}

26. Physical Activity Level-[POSITIVE]->Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases TDEE because more energy is expended during physical activity. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level directly reduces TDEE. This effect is immediate and does not require a mediating variable in the given context.', 'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'prob_std': 0.2324254312902623, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S216183132300217X'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7840446355825774, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper directly contradicts the claim. Ostendorf et al. report that differences in TDEE between groups were driven by higher physical activity energy expenditure (PAEE) and higher PAL/steps in weight‑loss maintainers; after adjusting for fat mass and fat‑free mass there were no between‑group differences in resting energy expenditure (REE). The authors therefore attribute higher TDEE to greater activity energy expenditure (and do not find evidence of a chronically elevated REE due to higher habitual activity). The study is also cross‑sectional, so it cannot demonstrate a permanent, activity‑induced elevation of BMR. Thus the citations do not support the asserted mechanism that PA raises TDEE entirely via long‑term increases in BMR independent of the immediate energy cost of activity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper (Pontzer et al., 2016) finds that total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) is not simply additive with physical activity: TDEE increases at low activity levels but plateaus at higher levels due to compensatory reductions in other expenditures. Crucially, the authors report that adjusted resting metabolic rate (RMR) was not correlated with physical activity (RMR_ADJ showed no association with CPM/d). They even cite long-term exercise studies showing reduced, not elevated, BMR in some cases. Thus the source does not support — and in parts directly contradicts — the claim that higher habitual physical activity produces a permanent elevation in BMR that keeps TDEE elevated independent of immediate activity costs."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S216183132300217X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no substantive content or evidence regarding the relationship between physical activity level and TDEE or long-term BMR changes. Because there is no usable citation text supporting the claim, the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level influences Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) by directly increasing basal metabolic rate (BMR); individuals who engage in higher levels of physical activity experience a permanent elevation in BMR, which means that their TDEE remains elevated even during prolonged periods of rest or inactivity. Therefore, changes in Physical Activity Level cause corresponding changes in TDEE entirely through long-term adaptations in resting metabolic function, independent of the immediate energy cost of the physical activity itself.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level influences Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) by directly increasing basal metabolic rate (BMR); individuals who engage in higher levels of physical activity experience a permanent elevation in BMR, which means that their TDEE remains elevated even during prolonged periods of rest or inactivity. Therefore, changes in Physical Activity Level cause corresponding changes in TDEE entirely through long-term adaptations in resting metabolic function, independent of the immediate energy cost of the physical activity itself.', 'perplexity': 1.2567841687823833, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8510049144730161, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.12695093677770683, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005248518499676183, 'prob_variance': 0.05402158111046444, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8978308212897732, 'avg_nll': 0.22855621143052984}

