ividuals\' physical activity levels drive the socio-cultural ideal for BMI, but it also does not explicitly study or rule out any causal effect of physical activity on cultural ideals. Thus the source supports the explanation that socio-cultural ideals are driven by cultural norms rather than by individual physical-activity behavior, but it does not directly demonstrate the absence of any causal pathway from physical activity to socio-cultural ideal BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK278977/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Endotext chapter emphasizes that socio-cultural norms and social/environmental factors shape body image and obesity-related outcomes (it cites cultural body-image differences and discusses social determinants). It also treats physical activity as an individual/behavioral factor affecting obesity prevalence, but it does not present evidence testing whether changes in individuals\' physical activity causally change socio-cultural ideals of BMI. Thus the sources support the idea that socio-cultural ideals are driven by social/cultural factors, but they do not provide direct evidence establishing the absence of any causal effect from physical activity on socio-cultural ideal BMI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Physical Activity Level does not cause Socio-cultural Ideal BMI to change in a direct causal relationship. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI is shaped by cultural norms and societal values, not by individuals' physical activity behaviors. There is no direct causal mechanism by which changes in Physical Activity Level alter Socio-cultural Ideal BMI.", 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3239161362203657, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8231337183903535, 'min_prob': 0.11611863104019225, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004765387610815271, 'prob_variance': 0.0684817020