30. Physical Activity Level-[NEGATIVE]->Basal Metabolic Rate
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change through direct physiological mechanisms. Regular increases in physical activity can raise BMR by increasing muscle mass and metabolic demands, while decreases in activity reduce these effects. Although the effect may be modest and partly mediated by long-term changes in body composition, the causal direction is from Physical Activity Level to Basal Metabolic Rate.', 'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/basal-metabolic-rate', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14692598/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article does not report that higher physical activity causes a long-term decrease in basal metabolic rate. It states that BMR is primarily determined by lean (fat‑free) mass (more muscle raises BMR), that aerobic fitness does not correlate with resting metabolism once body composition is accounted for, and that resistance (anaerobic) training tends to increase or at least not reduce resting energy expenditure. The article does note metabolic rate can fall with calorie restriction or illness, but it does not provide evidence that greater habitual physical activity causes the body to lower BMR to \\"conserve energy.\\" Thus the claim is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/basal-metabolic-rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page (no content accessible), so there is no evidence in the cited source to support the claim that higher physical activity causes a decrease in basal metabolic rate. Because the citation contains no usable content, it does not substantiate the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic page lists factors that influence BMR and states that more lean muscle (built by resistance/strength training) increases BMR. It notes BMR can decrease with age or with calorie restriction/fasting, but it does not state that higher habitual physical activity causes the body to slow resting metabolism. The source therefore does not support the claimed negative causal relationship; if anything it indicates increased muscle from exercise raises BMR.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14692598/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review reports that many human and animal studies show short-term or long-term increases in resting metabolic rate (RMR) with exercise (often via increased lean mass), with other studies finding no change; reductions in RMR are described as exceptions (extreme interventions or specific cases like flight). It does not support a general causal statement that higher physical activity causes lowered basal metabolic rate via energy-saving adaptation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in higher levels of physical activity causes the body to become more energy-efficient over time, which leads to a decrease in basal metabolic rate. As people exercise more, their bodies adapt by slowing down metabolism at rest to conserve energy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in higher levels of physical activity causes the body to become more energy-efficient over time, which leads to a decrease in basal metabolic rate. As people exercise more, their bodies adapt by slowing down metabolism at rest to conserve energy.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

