40. Norm BMI-[NEGATIVE]->Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Norm BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change in a direct causal relationship. There is no established mechanism by which a group's normative BMI directly alters an individual's energy intake. Any association is likely mediated by other variables such as social norms or individual behaviors, not a direct causal link.", 'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'citations': ['https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/proceedings-of-the-nutrition-society/article/metabolic-adaptations-during-negative-energy-balance-and-their-potential-impact-on-appetite-and-food-intake/92FC7CC3D00F801FBC1B8F4A164ADECD', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565815/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/proceedings-of-the-nutrition-society/article/metabolic-adaptations-during-negative-energy-balance-and-their-potential-impact-on-appetite-and-food-intake/92FC7CC3D00F801FBC1B8F4A164ADECD", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides evidence that body composition and resting metabolic rate (FFM → RMR) are positively associated with a tonic drive to eat, so reductions in metabolically active tissue and RMR tend to be associated with lower total energy intake. However, the review emphasizes FFM/RMR (not BMI per se) as the primary determinants, and it documents that increased metabolic “efficiency” (adaptive thermogenesis, greater muscular efficiency) occurs during weight loss and can be accompanied by increased orexigenic drive and compensatory eating. Thus the sources support a negative association between reduced metabolically active mass / RMR and TDEI, but do not clearly support the specific causal phrasing in the explanation that a lower (normal) BMI causes higher metabolic efficiency which then reduces TDEI; in fact the review describes situations where increased efficiency promotes increased intake."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review describes how resting metabolic rate and total energy expenditure are proportional to body mass (especially fat-free mass) and documents that energy requirements fall with weight loss (including adaptive reductions in RMR). It therefore supports the claim that lower body mass/BMI is associated with lower total daily energy needs (and that reductions in metabolism accompany weight loss). While the exact phrase \\"higher metabolic efficiency\\" isn\'t used, the paper explicitly describes the physiological reductions in energy expenditure that reduce required energy intake at lower body weight."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper does not support the claimed causal chain. Hume et al. report that low energy flux (low intake + low expenditure) predicts future fat gain and that higher resting metabolic rate (RMR) is associated with higher energy flux and less fat gain. They note metabolic adaptation (calorie restriction reduces RMR and increases metabolic efficiency), and that high-flux individuals often have higher TEI yet lose fat. Nothing in the paper shows that lower (normal) BMI causes higher metabolic efficiency which then lowers total daily energy intake; in fact the results and cited evidence point the opposite directions (e.g., higher RMR with higher flux and higher TEI in the group that lost fat). Therefore the explanation and proposed negative BMI → lower TDEI causal link are not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter shows that absolute resting and total energy expenditure tend to increase with body size/BMI (i.e., individuals with higher BMI usually have higher absolute TEE), which is consistent with the final statement that lower BMI is associated with lower TDEI in absolute terms. However, the source does not support the specific explanatory mechanism offered. It actually reports that (a) constitutionally thin people have lower absolute REE than normal-weight people, (b) when normalized to fat‑free mass some thin individuals show higher metabolic activity per unit FFM (not the same as overall \\"higher metabolic efficiency\\" causing lower energy needs), and (c) differences largely depend on body composition and are complex — the committee concluded evidence on BMI–REE interactions is limited. The citation therefore supports a general association of lower BMI with lower absolute energy expenditure, but not the causal claim that lower (normal) BMI increases metabolic efficiency which then reduces required daily energy intake.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565815/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter shows that total energy requirement/intake is driven by body size and activity — heavier (higher-BMI) people generally have higher maintenance metabolism and thus higher energy requirements/intake, so a negative association (lower BMI → lower TDEI) is supported. However, the explanation’s mechanism (that lower/normal BMI causes higher metabolic efficiency, thereby reducing TDEI) is not supported by the text. The source indicates obese subjects have higher maintenance metabolism and describes adaptive reductions in metabolism during energy restriction/weight los