   Properties: {'original_motivation': "'Healthy BMI' does not cause 'Norm BMI' because neither variable is defined, and there is no known direct causal link between them. Without definitions, measurement, or mechanisms, no causal relationship can be established in either direction.", 'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6469873/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review notes that clinicians often recommend eating less and exercising to address high BMI and that BMI can be used to guide progress, but it emphasizes numerous limitations: BMI is a crude screening measure, weight status is influenced by genetics, environment, fitness, body composition and population-specific factors, and outcomes vary. The source does not claim that maintaining a healthy BMI causally \\"shifts the body\'s metabolism to stabilize within the normative BMI range\\" nor that adopting healthier habits will always move and keep BMI in the normal range. Therefore the strong, deterministic causal claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6469873/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article critiques BMI as a measurement and emphasizes its conceptual ambiguity, limits for individual-level inference, and lack of clear biophysical mechanisms linking BMI to health. It discusses population-level associations (especially for severe obesity) but warns against assuming direct causal pathways or that BMI reliably maps to individual metabolic change. It does not provide evidence that the behaviors tied to achieving a \\"healthy BMI\\" universally or directly cause the body to stabilize within a normative BMI range or that BMI will \\"always\\" move toward and remain in the normal range. Thus the source does not support the causal claim or the strong, universal behavioral mechanism asserted."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article defines BMI, describes its categories, clinical significance, and associations with disease, and notes that clinicians may provide diet/exercise counseling. It does not provide evidence that the behaviors cited \\"directly cause\\" a metabolic shift that will reliably move and keep an individual\'s BMI within the normal range, nor does it claim an inevitable or permanent effect. The explanation\'s causal and absolute claims (metabolic stabilization and \\"will always move toward and remain\\" normal) are not supported by the cited content, which also highlights BMI\'s limitations and variability across individuals and populations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NASEM workshop chapter explains what BMI measures, its strengths and limitations, and discusses obesity pathophysiology and that obesity is a chronic condition requiring lifelong care. It does not provide evidence that maintaining a \\"healthy BMI\\" causally shifts metabolism so that an individual\'s BMI will always move into and remain in the normative range as a result of adopting exercise and balanced eating. The source emphasizes BMI is an imperfect measure, that risk varies by age/sex/ethnicity, and that obesity has complex, persistent drivers — contrary to the claim of inevitable, permanent normalization of BMI from