   Properties: {'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'prob_std': 0.2771072371744064, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.yalemedicine.org/news/why-you-shouldnt-rely-on-bmi-alone', 'https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html', 'https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/news/publications/health-matters/is-bmi-accurate'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8045371062426707, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article defines BMI, its uses and limitations, and notes that factors like activity level affect overall health. It does not address causal direction between BMI and physical activity, nor does it state that a healthy BMI does not cause changes in physical activity (or that physical activity typically causes BMI changes). Therefore the citation does not support the claimed absence of a causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.yalemedicine.org/news/why-you-shouldnt-rely-on-bmi-alone", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Yale Medicine piece describes BMI as a limited measurement and explicitly notes that BMI “varies … among people … whether they are active or sedentary,” which supports the common direction that physical activity influences BMI and that BMI is not itself a comprehensive driver of health status. However, the article does not explicitly state that a healthy BMI does not cause changes in physical activity (it does not directly address causality from BMI → activity). Therefore the citation supports the claimed typical direction (activity → BMI) and the idea that BMI isn’t a sole determinant, but it does not directly prove the absence of any causal effect in the reverse direction.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited American Cancer Society material describes BMI as a measure of weight and emphasizes that diet and physical activity influence weight (i.e., physical activity can affect BMI). It does not state or provide evidence that having a healthy BMI does not causally change a person\'s physical activity level. The source supports the reverse direction (physical activity → BMI) but is silent on, and does not rule out, any causal effect from BMI → physical activity. Therefore the claim that there is no causal relationship (NONE) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/news/publications/health-matters/is-bmi-accurate", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article argues that BMI is an imperfect measure and that health is driven by behaviors (including physical activity) and other factors, implying that activity influences health/BMI rather than BMI driving activity. However, it does not present direct evidence or studies showing that a healthy BMI does not causally change a person\'s physical activity level, nor does it explicitly test causal direction. It therefore weakly supports the claim but does not directly demonstrate absence of a causal effect."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy BMI does not cause Physical Activity Level to change. There is no direct causal relationship where having a healthy BMI leads to changes in how physically active a person is. Typically, it is Physical Activity Level that causally influences BMI, not the other way around.', 'aggreg