   Properties: {'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'prob_std': 0.24081749926763404, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8003130165110838, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article lists the determinants of BMR (body size, amount of lean muscle tissue, adipose tissue, age, sex, genetics, etc.) and emphasizes that body composition — not a simple label like “healthy BMI” — is what influences metabolic rate and that BMR can be changed mainly by increasing lean muscle. The article does not state that “healthy BMI” by itself directly causes changes in BMR; however it does note that weight/body size affect BMR, so changes in BMI that reflect changes in mass or composition could affect BMR. Because the source supports the claim that composition, age, and genetics matter more than a BMI label but does not explicitly state that a healthy BMI has no causal effect, the support is partial.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article shows BMR is driven mainly by body composition (especially fat‑free/lean mass), age, organ/tissue metabolic activity and physiological factors; it does not present BMI itself as a primary mechanistic driver. At the same time the article documents that predictive equations include total body mass and that changes in mass/composition (e.g., muscle gain, very low calorie diets) can alter metabolic rate. Because the sources support that composition and age — not the label “healthy BMI” per se — determine BMR, but also show that changes in body mass/composition (which affect BMI) can change BMR, the claim that “Healthy BMI does not cause BMR to change” is only partially supported by the citation content."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited page defines BMR and lists factors that influence it (age, weight, height, sex, dieting, exercise, etc.) but does not discuss BMI or state that having a healthy BMI does not affect BMR. Because the source neither asserts nor provides evidence that a “healthy BMI” has no causal effect on BMR, it does not support the claim of no relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy BMI does not cause Basal Metabolic Rate to change. Basal Metabolic Rate is determined primarily by factors such as body composition, age, and genetics, not by having a healthy BMI per se. There is no direct causal mechanism where achieving or maintaining a healthy BMI causes Basal Metabolic Rate to increase or decrease.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2503670734044212, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8580781510215492, 'min_prob': 0.11034780768700933, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002231566171444761, 'prob_variance': 0.05799306795351693, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8935138138924921, 'avg_nll': 0.2234371669284631}

