65. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI-[POSITIVE]->Norm BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI causes Norm BMI to change in a direct causal relationship. As the socio-cultural ideal for BMI increases, the socially accepted norm BMI tends to rise because people adjust perceptions of what is considered normal based on cultural ideals. Conversely, a lower socio-cultural ideal BMI leads to a decrease in norm BMI. This relationship is direct and does not require mediating variables from the list provided.', 'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'prob_std': 0.3088771314593597, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6469873/', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7162509034851211, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6469873/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper is a sociological critique of BMI as a measurement and how BMI, medical classification, and cultural norms performatively shape perceptions, policies, stigma, and behaviors. It documents how measurement and socio-cultural ideals can influence how bodies are labeled and experienced (e.g., internalization of BMI, performativity), but it does not provide evidence that shifting socio-cultural ideal BMI produces population-level physiological adaptation (e.g., changes in metabolic set points) that raise or lower Norm BMI. The claimed direct biological mechanism is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses BMI\'s history (originating from an idealized male body), its use and limitations, and difficulties inferring causation from BMI associations. It does not provide evidence that socio-cultural ideals produce physiological adaptation or shifting metabolic set points across populations, nor that changing ideals directly cause changes in average (norm) BMI. Therefore the biological causal mechanism claimed is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article discusses BMI\'s definition, history (including Quetelet\'s notion of an \\"average\\" or social ideal), and social aspects (stigma, legislation, usage), but it does not provide evidence that socio-cultural ideals produce population-level physiological adaptation or shifts in metabolic set points that change the population norm BMI. The specific causal biological mechanism asserted (bodies adjusting metabolic set points in response to shifting ideals) is not supported by the content of the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Socio-cultural Ideal BMI causes changes in Norm BMI through physiological adaptation at the population level. As the Socio-cultural Ideal BMI increases, individuals' bodies gradually adjust their metabolic set points over time to align with these ideals, resulting in a higher average Norm BMI in society. Conversely, when the Socio-cultural Ideal BMI decreases, widespread physiological changes lead to a reduction in Norm BMI. This direct biological response to shifting ideals ensures that population norms reflect prevailing socio-cultural standards for BMI.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Socio-cultural Ideal BMI causes changes in Norm BMI through physiological adaptation at the population level. As the Socio-cultural Ideal BMI increases, individuals' bodies gradually adjust their metabolic set points over time to align 