   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) causes Group-level BMI to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing TDEE (through higher physical activity or metabolic rate) leads to greater energy being burned, which directly reduces group-level BMI if energy intake is held constant. Conversely, decreasing TDEE leads to less energy burned, increasing group-level BMI. The causal direction is from TDEE to Group-level BMI.', 'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes what TEE/TDEE is and shows that higher absolute TEE is typically a consequence of larger body size and greater fat‑free mass (i.e., people with higher BMI often have higher absolute TEE). It also documents compensatory reductions in energy expenditure that follow weight loss (adaptive thermogenesis). It does not provide evidence that higher TDEE causes bodies to store more fat and thereby increases group‑level BMI; on the contrary, the source emphasizes that TEE often rises with body size and that differences in TEE between groups disappear after adjusting for body composition. Therefore the causal claim (higher TDEE → greater fat storage → higher BMI) is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes what TDEE is, notes that energy expenditure influences appetite/energy intake and that obese people tend to burn more energy because of greater body mass (reverse association). It also presents the constrained energy-expenditure model and recommends increasing activity to raise expenditure for weight loss. It does not provide evidence that higher TDEE causally causes bodies to store more fat and raise group-level BMI; at best it notes increased expenditure can drive increased intake (an indirect and conditional effect), but does not support the claimed direct compensatory increase in fat storage and BMI. Therefore the sources do not support the asserted positive causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "A higher Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) means that individuals are burning more calories throughout the day, which should cause their bodies to adapt by storing more energy as fat, thereby increasing BMI at the group level. Therefore, as TDEE increases, group-level BMI should also increase due to the body's tendency to compensate for high energy usage.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "A higher Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) means that individuals are burning more calories throughout the day, which should cause their bodies to adapt by storing more energy as fat, thereby increasing BMI at the group level. Therefore, as TDEE increases, group-level BMI should also increase due to the body's tendency to compensate for high energy usage.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

