   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Group-level BMI in a direct causal relationship. Group-level BMI is typically calculated as the aggregate (e.g., mean or median) of individual BMIs within a group. Therefore, an increase in an individual's BMI will directly increase the Group-level BMI, while a decrease will lower it.", 'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article defines BMI and describes its use for individuals and population-level summaries, but it contains no claim that an individual\'s BMI is inversely related to a group\'s average BMI. Nowhere in the source is the assertion that one person\'s BMI rising implies others must fall (or that an individual\'s higher BMI reduces the group average) supported. The article treats BMI as a component of group statistics (e.g., population averages) in the usual additive sense, not as negatively related to group-level BMI as claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses what BMI measures, population distributions, trends, and familial/spousal clustering of BMI (positive associations within groups). It does not state or provide evidence that an individual\'s BMI is inversely related to the group\'s average BMI; if anything it documents clustering and population-level increases. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the cited source(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews the uses and limitations of BMI at individual and population levels, noting that BMI can summarize population burdens and track trends, but it does not state or provide evidence that an individual’s BMI is inversely related to the group\'s average BMI. Nothing in the source supports the claim that a higher individual BMI would reduce the group-level BMI; the explanation asserts a statistical relationship (an individual increase forcing others lower to keep the mean stable) that the citation does not describe or justify."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page describes what BMI is, how it\'s used for individual screening and population-level measurement, and gives guidance and data uses. It does not state or provide evidence that an individual\'s BMI is inversely related to the group\'s average BMI or that increases in one person\'s BMI imply others must decrease to keep the group average stable. The explanation\'s claim about a negative relationship is not supported by the content of the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "An individual's Body Mass Index (BMI) is often inversely related to the group's average BMI because when one person's BMI increases, it suggests that others in the group must have lower BMIs to keep the group average stable. Therefore, a higher individual BMI would actually reduce the overall group-level BMI.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "An individual's Body Mass Index (BMI) is often inversely related to the group's average BMI because when one person's BMI increases, it suggests that others in the group must have lower BMIs to keep the group average stable. Therefore, a higher individual BMI would actually reduce the overall group-level BMI.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

