   Properties: {'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'prob_std': 0.2803288636568856, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8232225388679952, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC page describes BMI as a calculated measure of weight relative to height, used for screening and tracking weight change, and states that weight/obesity are influenced by health behaviors (diet, physical activity) and other factors. This supports the idea that energy intake and behaviors influence BMI (TDEI → BMI). The source does not, however, explicitly address or rule out any causal effect of BMI on daily energy intake, nor does it explicitly state “BMI does not cause TDEI.” Therefore the citation supports the claimed causal direction indirectly but does not directly substantiate the categorical claim of no causal effect in the reverse direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page defines BMI as a calculated measure of weight relative to height and describes its use as a screening indicator and health risk correlate, but it does not discuss causal relationships between BMI and Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) or state that BMI does not cause changes in energy intake. The source therefore does not provide evidence for the claimed causal direction (TDEI → BMI) or for the assertion that BMI does not directly influence daily energy consumption."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes BMI as a screening/measurement tool that estimates body fat from height and weight and outlines its limitations. That supports the idea that BMI is an indicator (a measurement/consequence) rather than an active determinant of other behaviors. However, the source does not discuss or provide evidence about causal relationships between BMI and Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) or explicitly state that TDEI causes BMI and not vice versa. Therefore the citation only partially supports the claim that BMI does not cause TDEI."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Stanford Medicine article discusses what BMI measures, its limitations, and the difficulty of inferring causation from BMI associations (e.g., with mortality), and notes that diet and exercise are confounders. It does not provide evidence or direct discussion about whether BMI causally affects daily energy intake (TDEI) or explicitly state that the causal direction is from TDEI to BMI. Therefore the source does not support the specific claim that BMI does not cause TDEI or that causation runs from TDEI to BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review emphasizes that BMI is an index (a consequence of weight/height and long-term energy balance), discusses diet, food availability and energy intake as drivers of population BMI changes, and highlights BMI’s limitations as a measure of body fat — supporting the view that TDEI → BMI. However, the paper also describes psychosocial effects of body fatness (body image, societal pressures) that influence eating habits, which indicates potential feedback from body mass/perception to intake. The source therefore supports the direction TDEI → BMI but does not fully justify an absolute “no causal effect” (BMI → TDEI).  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) does not cause Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change in a direct causal relationship. BMI is a consequence of long-term energy intake and expenditure, but does not directly influence how much energy a person consumes each day. The causal direction is from TDEI to BMI, not the other way around.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3420391378051053, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8382175633745366, 'min_prob': 0.07210892915666148, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002789447742824015, 'prob_variance': 0.07858427179916075, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.922909257063235, 'avg_nll': 0.29419020191343415}

93. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NONE]->Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)
   Properties: {'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'prob_std': 0.17977518089552053, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-27246-z', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10445668/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8778580716658074, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that differences in TDEE are tied to components (resting energy expenditure and physical activity energy expenditure) and to body mass/composition, and it explicitly discusses that changes in TDEE with weight loss occur via reductions in REE and PAEE (and that PAEE scales with body weight). That supports the idea that BMI/body mass influences TDEE through mediators. However, the paper does not provide direct evidence proving the absence of any direct causal pathway from BMI to TDEE (and even discusses complex/adaptive effects on REE), so it does not fully substantiate the stronger claim that there is no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10445668/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper shows that total energy expenditure (TEE/TDEE) is strongly dependent on body mass/BMI and that TEE is composed of basal (BEE/BMR) and activity (AEE) components. It presents evidence and discussion that increases in body size change the energy cost of movement (affecting AEE) and that variation in BEE/BMR also drives TEE changes — consistent with a mediated pathway. However, the paper does not directly assert or demonstrate that BMI has no direct causal effect on TEE independent of those components; it reports associations and decomposes TEE into mediating components but cannot rule out (or directly test) a remaining direct effect. Therefore the citation supports the mediation rationale but does not fully support the stronger claim that there is absolutely no direct causal link from BMI to TDEE."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes TEE as the sum of REE (BMR), TEF, and physical activity and repeatedly shows that body size/composition and activity determine those components. It documents that higher BMI is associated with higher absolute REE and TEE but that differences often disappear after adjusting for fat‑free mass or body weight, indicating BMI effects operate through body composition and activity. The source does not explicitly assert a categorical statement that \\"BMI cannot directly cause TDEE to change,\\" but it supports the explanation that BMI acts via mediators (REE/BMR and physical activity) rather than being an independent causal driver. Therefore the claim is supported in principle, though the source does not strictly rule out any direct effect. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-27246-z", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Nature Communications paper shows that total energy expenditure (TEE/TEE adjusted for body composition) is repeatable in adults and finds no within- or among-individual correlation between TEE and body fat in the studied intervals. The paper also frames TEE as the sum of basal metabolic processes and physical activity and demonstrates that TEE covaries with fat-free mass and fat mass (and is analyzed after adjusting for those). These results are consistent with the idea that body composition (components of BMI) relates to TEE via underlying components (basal metabolism, activity) rather than showing BMI directly driving short-term changes in TEE. However, the study does not directly test or demonstrate a causal mediation pathway from BMI → (BMR, activity) → TEE, nor does it explicitly rule out all direct causal effects of BMI on TEE. Therefore the citation supports the explanation only partially."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) does not directly cause Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change. The causal pathway operates through mediating variables such as Basal Metabolic Rate and Physical Activity Level, which are already included in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship from BMI to TDEE.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1313727128832451, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9135120828521136, 'min_prob': 0.15871255039665874, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00146372467416053, 'prob_variance': 0.03231911566601713, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0241424461473967, 'avg_nll': 0.12343168562586151}

