   Properties: {'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'prob_std': 0.30378100513463097, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://www.betterhealth.vic.gov.au/health/healthyliving/body-mass-index-bmi'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6957686939438947, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes what BMI is, how it’s calculated, its uses as a screening tool, and limitations for assessing individual body fat and health risk. It contains no information about socio-cultural ideals, cultural norms, media influence, group-level feedbacks, or any causal (or non-causal) relationship between individual BMI and population-level socio-cultural ideals. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence for the claim that individual BMI does not directly cause Socio-cultural Ideal BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page describes what BMI is, how it\'s used for individual and population health screening, and its measurement limits; it does not discuss socio-cultural ideals, how cultural norms form, or causal relationships between individual BMI and collective body-size ideals. Nothing in the citation supports the specific claim that individual BMI does or does not directly cause socio-cultural ideal BMI or that cultural/media/historical factors (rather than individual BMI) shape those ideals."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses the historical and social origins of BMI (originating as a way to capture a \\"socially ideal male body\\") and emphasizes cultural, clinical, and institutional influences on how BMI is used and understood. That supports the idea that socio‑cultural ideals are shaped by broader historical and institutional factors rather than being a simple direct effect of an individual\'s BMI. However, the source does not explicitly analyze or provide evidence about causal pathways between individual BMI and socio‑cultural ideals, nor does it show that any observed associations are mediated by group‑level factors. Therefore the citation only partially supports the stated claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly discusses how ideals of body fatness have varied across cultures and history (paleolithic figurines, Egyptian art, 1920s flapper era) and attributes contemporary body-ideal pressures to societal norms, the fashion industry, and advertising rather than to individuals\' BMI. It treats BMI as an epidemiologic metric with social consequences but does not present a direct causal mechanism whereby an individual\'s BMI changes the socio-cultural ideal; instead it emphasizes group-level, cultural, historical, and media influences. This aligns with the claim that there is no direct causal link from BMI to Socio-cultural Ideal BMI and that any association is mediated by broader cultural factors."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) does not directly cause Socio-cultural Ideal BMI to change. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI reflects collective beliefs and norms about desirable body size, which are shaped by broader cultural, historical, and media factors rather than individual BMI values. Any association between BMI and Socio-cultural Ideal BMI is likely mediated by group-level factors or cultural feedbacks, not a direct causal mechanism from individual BMI.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.550649225287177, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7557505218591113, 'min_prob': 0.014131433484770597, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003898610502845331, 'prob_variance': 0.09228289908060668, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2771314906776794, 'avg_nll': 0.43867369825986186}

96. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NEGATIVE]->Physical Activity Level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Physical Activity Level to change in an inverse relationship. Higher BMI can directly lead to lower physical activity due to increased physical discomfort or mobility limitations, while lower BMI may make physical activity easier. This causal pathway is not mediated by other variables in the given set.', 'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'prob_std': 0.28976955084219364, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7374668908255183, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports associations between higher BMI and poorer physical fitness (explosive power, cardiorespiratory endurance) in university students and discusses possible behavioral and biomechanical explanations (extra load, avoidance, psychosocial factors). It does not measure or report physical activity level directly, nor does it present evidence that BMI acts via basal metabolic rate or elevated energy reserves to reduce a physiological drive to be active. It also does not support the claim that the effect is direct and independent of behavioral or environmental factors. Therefore the specific mechanistic claim is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic page explains what BMI measures, notes that body fat serves as an energy reserve, and lists health risks associated with high or low BMI. It does not provide evidence that BMI directly alters basal metabolic rate in a way that reduces physiological drive to be active, nor does it claim a direct, independent negative causal effect of higher BMI on physical activity levels. The specific mechanisms and causal link described in the explanation (impact on basal metabolic rate and reduced motivation for activity independent of other factors) are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited American Cancer Society page defines BMI, explains categories and limitations, and provides guidance on diet and physical activity, but it does not present evidence that BMI causally reduces physical activity via effects on basal metabolic rate or a lowered physiological drive to expend energy. The source does not describe the proposed direct mechanism or claim that the effect is independent of behavioral or environmental factors."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses what BMI measures and its limitations, associations with body fat, and some physiological consequences of adipose tissue (inflammation, insulin resistance, effects on muscle). It does not provide evidence that higher BMI directly lowers physical activity level via increased basal metabolic rate or \\"elevated energy reserves\\" that reduce physiological drive to be active, nor that lower BMI increases motivation to be active. The source also emphasizes that BMI\'s health associations vary by age, sex, ethnicity and that social/clinical factors matter, which contradicts the claim that the effect is independent of behavioral or environmental factors."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) exerts a direct influence on Physical Activity Level through its impact on basal metabolic rate. Individuals with higher BMI have elevated energy reserves, which reduces the physiological drive to engage in physical activity as a means of energy balance maintenance, whereas those with lower BMI experience increased motivation to be physically active to sustain adequate energy stores. This direct effect is independent of other behavioral or environmental factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) exerts a direct influence on Physical Activity Level through its impact on basal metabolic rate. Individuals with higher BMI have elevated energy reserves, which reduces the physiological drive to engage in physical activity as a means of energy balance maintenance, whereas those with lower BMI experience increased motivation to be physically active to sustain adequate energy stores. This direct effect is independent of other behavioral or environmental factors.', 'perplexity': 1.4563724717954254, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7808830704980693, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.11788881282934262, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006447081855207094, 'prob_variance': 0.08396639259528663, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.365748210659962, 'avg_nll': 0.37594873559932485}

