   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change in a direct causal relationship. As BMI increases—reflecting greater body mass—Basal Metabolic Rate typically increases because a larger body requires more energy to maintain basic physiological functions. Conversely, a decrease in BMI leads to a lower Basal Metabolic Rate. This causal effect is due to the direct link between total body mass and metabolic energy expenditure.', 'session_id': '8ee8779a-018a-4855-b516-5f400e9e99e5', 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic article states that lean muscle raises BMR because muscle requires more energy to maintain, and that adipose tissue is metabolically less active than muscle — supporting the mechanism in the explanation. However, the same source also says larger body size (more tissue/cells) increases BMR and provides equations where weight contributes positively to estimated BMR. It does not support a simple negative relationship between BMI and BMR (i.e., higher BMI causing lower BMR); instead it presents a nuanced view: fat is less metabolically active than muscle, but greater total mass generally increases BMR. Therefore the mechanism is supported but the claim of an overall negative BMI→BMR relationship is not."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article states BMR is directly proportional to lean (fat‑free) mass and that total body mass (used in common prediction equations) generally predicts higher BMR with greater mass. It also notes fat mass explains a small positive portion of BMR variation (not a negative one). The source does not claim that higher BMI (or greater adiposity) causes BMR to decrease; indeed, formulas and data in the article imply higher body mass typically associates with higher absolute BMR and that lean mass raises BMR. Therefore the explanation (higher BMI → lower BMR) is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher Body Mass Index (BMI) indicates a greater proportion of body fat, which is metabolically less active compared to lean muscle mass. Therefore, as BMI increases, the Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) actually decreases because the body requires less energy to maintain fat tissue than muscle.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher Body Mass Index (BMI) indicates a greater proportion of body fat, which is metabolically less active compared to lean muscle mass. Therefore, as BMI increases, the Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) actually decreases because the body requires less energy to maintain fat tissue than muscle.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

