how that a larger spatial scale (i.e., more widely distributed amenities increasing travel distances) promotes healthier BMI; in fact longer commuting distance was positively related to BMI. Thus the motivation\'s causal claim that increased distances from a larger spatial scale lead to healthier BMI is contradicted by the cited results."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Larger spatial scale in urban planning has been shown to directly promote healthier BMI by encouraging more distributed amenities, which increases the distance people must walk daily. This increased physical activity is thought to directly cause residents to maintain healthier body weight.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Larger spatial scale in urban planning has been shown to directly promote healthier BMI by encouraging more distributed amenities, which increases the distance people must walk daily. This increased physical activity is thought to directly cause residents to maintain healthier body weight.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

