16. Individual Ideal BMI-[NEGATIVE]->Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Individual Ideal BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change. Ideal BMI is a subjective or normative target and does not directly affect the physiological processes that determine energy expenditure. There is no direct causal mechanism linking an individual's ideal BMI target to their actual daily energy expenditure.", 'session_id': '778ac8f0-03b0-4476-b871-16576cfe9b1c', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565815/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review states that resting metabolic rate and the energy cost of movement are proportional to body mass (especially fat‑free mass) and that increasing body mass (becoming obese) raises total energy expenditure. It explicitly notes that becoming obese increases TDEE as an adaptive way to raise energy throughput. Thus the sources contradict, rather than support, the claim that a higher (ideal) BMI would lead to lower daily energy expenditure."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review reports that resting energy expenditure (the largest component of TEE) and absolute total daily energy expenditure tend to increase with greater body size/BMI. Studies summarized show higher absolute REE and TEE in individuals with obesity (and higher BMI), and higher absolute activity energy expenditure, with differences typically disappearing only after adjusting for fat‑free mass or body weight. The sources do not support a general negative relationship (higher ideal BMI → lower TDEE); rather they describe the opposite pattern or explain lower per‑kg or adjusted values under specific conditions."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565815/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter states that total energy expenditure is driven by body size and activity: overweight/obese individuals generally have higher maintenance metabolism (BMR) because they have greater fat‑free mass, and overall energy requirement is typically higher in heavier people. While obese individuals may move less, their greater body mass often produces similar or even higher activity‑induced energy expenditure. The source therefore contradicts the claim that carrying more weight makes the body require less energy (and that higher ideal BMI → lower TDEE). (The citation also does not specifically address “ideal BMI” as a preference.)"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "As an individual's ideal BMI increases, their total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) decreases because carrying more body weight is thought to make the body more efficient and require less energy for daily activities. Therefore, people with a higher ideal BMI supposedly burn fewer calories each day.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "As an individual's ideal BMI increases, their total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) decreases because carrying more body weight is thought to make the body more efficient and require less energy for daily activities. Therefore, people with a higher ideal BMI supposedly burn fewer calories each day.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

