20. Individual Ideal BMI-[POSITIVE]->Body Mass Index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Individual Ideal BMI does not directly cause Body Mass Index (BMI) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby a person's ideal or desired BMI automatically alters their actual BMI without mediating factors such as behavior or physiological changes. Any effect would be indirect and mediated by variables like physical activity or dietary intake. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'session_id': '778ac8f0-03b0-4476-b871-16576cfe9b1c', 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC material explains what BMI is, how it\'s used, and lists behavioral and medical risk factors for obesity, but it does not discuss individuals\' \\"ideal BMI,\\" personal weight ideals, or evidence that personal ideals causally change eating/exercise habits and thereby actual BMI. The specific causal mechanism claimed is not addressed by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses BMI as a measurement tool, its limitations, and factors like genetics, environment, fitness, and weight stigma that influence weight. It does not provide evidence or analysis linking an individual\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" or personal weight standard to subsequent changes in their actual BMI (no studies or data showing that higher personal ideal BMI causes people to subconsciously eat more or exercise less and thereby gain weight)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes BMI, its history (including Quetelet\'s idea of an \\"average\\" or social ideal) and social aspects of weight, but it does not provide empirical evidence that an individual\'s stated \\"ideal BMI\\" causally leads them to change eating/exercise and thereby raise their actual BMI. The specific causal psychological mechanism asserted in the motivation is not supported or documented in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes what BMI is, its categories, health effects and physiological associations, but it does not discuss individual \\"ideal BMI,\\" personal ideals or body‑image beliefs, nor any evidence that people adjust eating/exercise to match a personally held ideal BMI. The cited source therefore does not address the proposed causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited workshop chapter discusses what BMI measures, its strengths and limitations, and issues of stigma and communication about weight, but it does not present evidence or analysis linking an individual\'s ideal BMI (their personal preferred/ideal weight) to subsequent changes in their actual BMI or behaviors that would cause weight gain."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having a higher individual ideal BMI causes an increase in actual Body Mass Index (BMI) because people subconsciously adjust their eating and exercise habits to align with their personal ideal. As a result, those who believe their ideal BMI is higher will tend to gain weight to match this standard.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having a higher individual ideal BMI causes an increase in actual Body Mass Index (BMI) because people subconsciously adjust their eating and exercise habits to align with their personal ideal. As a result, those who believe their ideal BMI is higher will tend to gain weight to match this standard.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

