   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change through direct physiological mechanisms. Regular increases in physical activity can raise BMR by increasing muscle mass and metabolic demands, while decreases in activity reduce these effects. Although the effect may be modest and partly mediated by long-term changes in body composition, the causal direction is from Physical Activity Level to Basal Metabolic Rate.', 'session_id': '778ac8f0-03b0-4476-b871-16576cfe9b1c', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/basal-metabolic-rate', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14692598/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article does not support the claim that greater physical activity lowers BMR. It reports that aerobic fitness shows no correlation with resting metabolic rate when adjusted for fat‑free mass (studies from 1992 and 1997), and that resistance/weight training and increased lean mass tend to increase resting energy expenditure. The sources therefore contradict the asserted causal link that higher activity produces an adaptive decrease in basal metabolic rate."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/basal-metabolic-rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and contains no usable content or evidence. Therefore the claim about higher physical activity causing a long-term decrease in basal metabolic rate is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The Cleveland Clinic article does not support that greater physical activity lowers BMR. It states BMR is largely fixed by factors like age, sex, body size and lean muscle mass, and specifically notes building lean muscle (via resistance/strength training) increases BMR. The article lists situations that lower BMR (e.g., fasting) but gives no evidence that higher habitual physical activity causes a long‑term reduction in basal metabolic rate."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14692598/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review reports that many human and animal studies find short-term increases (EPOC) and that long-term training often increases or has no effect on resting metabolic rate (often via increased lean mass). Only extreme interventions are noted to sometimes reduce RMR. Thus the citation does not support a general negative effect of higher physical activity on basal metabolic rate."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in higher levels of physical activity causes the body to become more energy-efficient over time, which leads to a decrease in basal metabolic rate. As people exercise more, their bodies adapt by slowing down metabolism at rest to conserve energy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in higher levels of physical activity causes the body to become more energy-efficient over time, which leads to a decrease in basal metabolic rate. As people exercise more, their bodies adapt by slowing down metabolism at rest to conserve energy.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

