/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review states that resting metabolic rate and total energy requirements are proportional to body mass (especially fat‑free mass) and that metabolism and energy requirements decline with loss of body mass (citing reductions in RMR after weight loss of ~170–480 kcal/day). Thus lower body mass/BMI is associated with lower total daily energy needs, supporting the proposed negative relationship. The exact phrasing “higher metabolic efficiency” is not used, but the paper documents the same causal link (lower BMI → reduced energy expenditure → lower TDEI)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents metabolic adaptation: caloric restriction and weight loss can produce increased metabolic efficiency (reduced RMR and other energy‑sparing responses), which would lower energy requirements — supporting the proposed mechanism in principle. However, the study does not provide direct evidence that a lower or normal BMI per se causes higher metabolic efficiency or that BMI is negatively associated with habitual total daily energy intake. In fact, the paper emphasizes that TEI alone did not predict fat gain and shows complex relations between energy flux, RMR, and TEI (e.g., high‑flux individuals had higher TEI yet lost fat). Thus the mechanistic element is supported, but the specific causal claim linking lower/normal BMI to reduced TDEI is not directly demonstrated."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review shows that resting energy expenditure (REE) and total energy expenditure (TEE) tend to increase with body size/BMI and that absolute daily energy expenditure is higher in people with obesity than in those without obesity—consistent with lower BMI being associated with lower TDEI. However, the source does not clearly support the specific causal claim that a lower (normal) BMI produces greater \\"metabolic efficiency\\" which then reduces energy needs. The report highlights heterogeneity, compensatory/adaptive changes after weight loss, and inconsistent findings (e.g., constitutionally thin individuals may show higher metabolic activity when normalized to fat‑free mass), and concludes evidence on BMI–REE interactions and mechanisms is limited."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565815/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review supports the general association that total daily energy requirement (and thus intake) is driven by body size/composition — overweight individuals have higher maintenance metabolism and higher energy requirements, so lower BMI is associated with lower total energy needs. However, the paper does not support the specific mechanistic claim that a lower (normal) BMI \\"causes people to have higher metabolic efficiency\\" that reduces TDEI. If anything, the review emphasizes maintenance metabolism is determined mainly by fat‑free mass (larger in overweight people) and describes adaptive reductions in metabolism with weight loss (i.e., increased efficiency after weight loss), which contradicts the asserted mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A lower (normal) BMI causes people to have higher metabolic efficiency, which in turn reduces their need for total daily energy intake. Therefore, as BMI decreases into the normal range, total daily energy intake also decreases.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A lower (normal) BMI causes 