hey primarily describe interactive and mediated relationships. Hence the motivation\'s claim that only indirect, mediated effects occur is supported in spirit by the citations, but not conclusively or unambiguously proven."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes components and determinants of energy expenditure (resting metabolic rate, activity, thermic effect of food) and notes relationships like weight change affecting expenditure and energy expenditure influencing energy intake. It does not provide evidence or analysis showing that total daily energy intake does or does not directly cause changes in physical activity level, nor that any effects are specifically mediated by BMR or BMI. Therefore the citation does not address the asserted absence of a direct causal pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S216183132300217X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error message and provides no content. Therefore the provided citation does not supply any evidence to support or contradict the claimed lack of a direct causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) does not cause Physical Activity Level to change in a direct causal manner. While energy intake may indirectly influence the capacity for activity via energy availability or body weight, these effects are mediated by other variables such as Basal Metabolic Rate or Body Mass Index. There is no direct causal pathway from TDEI to Physical Activity Level in the absence of mediators. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3858048776018423, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8100975