   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change in a direct causal relationship. As BMI increases—reflecting greater body mass—Basal Metabolic Rate typically increases because a larger body requires more energy to maintain basic physiological functions. Conversely, a decrease in BMI leads to a lower Basal Metabolic Rate. This causal effect is due to the direct link between total body mass and metabolic energy expenditure.', 'session_id': '778ac8f0-03b0-4476-b871-16576cfe9b1c', 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Cleveland Clinic page states that fat (adipose) tissue is less metabolically active than muscle, but still requires energy, and that larger body size/weight increases BMR (the provided equations have positive weight terms). It also notes muscle increases BMR. The source does not say that higher BMI leads to a lower BMR; on the contrary, it indicates BMR rises with body mass and is higher with more lean muscle. Therefore the claim that higher BMI causes BMR to decrease is contradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article states BMR is directly proportional to lean (fat-free) mass and that increasing muscle mass raises BMR. Predictive equations and evidence in the source show total body mass (and especially fat-free mass) explain most variation in BMR; fat mass explains a smaller but positive portion (~7% in one study). While the source notes adipose is less metabolically active than muscle, it does not support the cl