   Properties: {'session_id': '778ac8f0-03b0-4476-b871-16576cfe9b1c', 'prob_std': 0.17977518089552053, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-27246-z', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10445668/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8778580716658074, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study defines TDEE as the sum of resting energy expenditure (REE), the thermic effect of feeding, and physical activity energy expenditure (PAEE), and explicitly states that changes in TDEE with weight loss occur because REE and PAEE decline primarily from reductions in body mass. The paper\'s results attribute between-group differences in TDEE to differences in PAEE (and to REE only insofar as it relates to fat mass/fat‑free mass), showing that higher PAEE drives higher TDEE in weight‑loss maintainers despite similar BMI to controls. Thus the citations support the claim that BMI influences TDEE through mediators (REE/PAEE/physical activity) rather than as a direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10445668/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper reports that TEE, BEE and AEE are dependent on body mass/BMI and presents data showing BMI/body mass relate to components of energy expenditure (and that TEE = BEE + AEE). It documents how increases in body size change the energy cost of movement (AEE) and influence basal expenditure, i.e. BMI/body mass are associated with TEE (via its components). Thus the sources do not support a claim of no causal connection; rather they describe BMI/body mass as a predictor of TEE through BEE and AEE (and report measurable associations), contradicting the asserted \\"NONE\\" relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited report defines TEE as the sum of resting (REE/BMR), thermic effect of food, and activity components and repeatedly identifies body size/composition (and thus BMI) as a determinant of REE and of activity energy expenditure. That supports the motivation’s claim that BMI influences TDEE via mediating components (BMR/physical activity). However, the source also reports empirical associations between BMI/obesity and absolute TEE (and notes a linear relationship between increasing BMI and REE) and does not explicitly assert that BMI has no independent/direct effect on TEE outside those components. Therefore the citations support the mediator pathway but do not fully rule out or explicitly deny any direct BMI→TDEE effect."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-27246-z", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that total energy expenditure (TEE/TDEE) is largely explained by body composition (fat‑free mass and fat mass) and that adjusted TEE is repeatable across adults, indicating that composition and individual metabolic rate profiles drive TEE variation. It also notes changes in fat‑free mass relate to changes in adjusted TEE and explicitly acknowledges lack of direct physical activity and resting/basal metabolic rate measures. However, the paper does not directly test or establish a causal null effect of BMI on TDEE, nor does it provide direct measures of basal metabolic rate or activity to demonstrate mediation empirically. Thus the citation supports the idea that composition/physiology (rather than BMI per se) are the proximate determinants of TEE, but it does not definitively demonstrate the absence of any direct causal path from BMI to TDEE."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) does not directly cause Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change. The causal pathway operates through mediating variables such as Basal Metabolic Rate and Physical Activity Level, which are already included in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship from BMI to TDEE.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1313727128832451, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9135120828521136, 'min_prob': 0.15871255039665874, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00146372467416053, 'prob_variance': 0.03231911566601713, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0241424461473967, 'avg_nll': 0.12343168562586151}

