103. Group-level BMI-[NEGATIVE]->Norm BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI through the process of social comparison and norm-setting. As the average BMI within a group increases, the perceived 'normal' BMI (Norm BMI) also tends to increase, since individuals adjust their expectations based on the group average. Conversely, a decrease in group-level BMI lowers the norm BMI. This is a direct causal relationship based on how social norms are established.", 'session_id': '778ac8f0-03b0-4476-b871-16576cfe9b1c', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3633251/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses BMI as a measurement tool, its limits, and that obesity-related stigma exists and can harm health, but it provides no evidence or discussion that higher group-level BMI produces social pressure that causes individuals to lower their own BMI or adopt healthier lifestyles, nor that rising average BMI leads to a reduced norm BMI. The specific causal mechanism in the motivation is not addressed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls citation describes BMI definitions, clinical implications, physiology, and health risks of higher BMI but contains no evidence or discussion of social-pressure, social-comparison, group-level BMI effects on individual motivation, or changes in normative BMI driven by social dynamics. The causal social-behavioral mechanism asserted in the motivation is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses BMI measurement issues, historical trends (mean BMI has risen over decades), and social attitudes/stigma around fatness (peer pressure, cultural ideals of thinness), but it does not provide evidence that higher group-level BMI causes individuals to feel pressure to reduce their own BMI or that rising population BMI leads to a subsequent reduction in the normative individual BMI. In fact, the paper documents secular increases in mean BMI, not a population-level decline driven by the mechanism proposed."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3633251/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines how individual deviation above or below an ethnic group\'s BMI norm relates to psychosocial maladjustment (stigma, lower self-worth, peer status) and finds that individuals who deviate toward higher BMI experience worse outcomes. It does not provide evidence that increases in group-level BMI cause group members to feel pressure to maintain a lower BMI, nor that rising average BMI motivates people to adopt healthier lifestyles and thereby lower the norm over time. The causal mechanism and negative effect claimed in the motivation are not tested or reported in the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses BMI\'s strengths/limits, population cutoffs, and weight stigma (social devaluation and discrimination) and notes that stigma can impede help-seeking and weight-loss maintenance. It does not provide evidence that increases in group-level BMI cause individuals to feel pressure to maintain a lower BMI or that rising average BMI motivates people to adopt healthier lifestyles and thereby reduce the norm BMI. The proposed causal social-comparison mechanism is not examined or supported in the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in group-level BMI causes individuals within the group to feel social pressure to maintain a lower BMI in order to stand out or avoid negative health comparisons, leading to a reduction in the norm BMI. As the average BMI rises, people may be more motivated to adopt healthier lifestyles, resulting in a lower norm BMI over time.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in group-level BMI causes individuals within the group to feel social pressure to maintain a lower BMI in order to stand out or avoid negative health comparisons, leading to a reduction in the norm BMI. As the average BMI rises, people may be more motivated to adopt healthier lifestyles, resulting in a lower norm BMI over time.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

