ocial norms influencing ideal body weight. Therefore the motivational claim is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses the uses, limits, and societal effects of BMI (including weight stigma and population trends) but does not present evidence or analysis showing that higher group-level BMI causes individuals to set lower personal ideal BMIs or to distinguish themselves from group norms. The specific behavioral/social-comparison mechanism claimed is not addressed in the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses BMI measurement, historical and cultural ideals of body size, and notes that socially desired (\\"desirable\\") weight has often been lower than the population average. However it does not provide evidence that increases in group-level BMI cause individuals to adopt lower personal ideal BMIs (nor that people lower their ideals to distinguish themselves from group norms). The specific dynamic negative relationship claimed is not examined or supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "When the average BMI of a group increases, individuals within that group are likely to set their personal ideal BMI lower in an attempt to counterbalance the group's trend. This is because people generally strive to distinguish themselves from the prevailing group norm, so a higher group-level BMI would motivate individuals to adopt a lower ideal BMI.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "When the average BMI of a group increases, individuals within that group are likely to set their perso